window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2016-06-1", "source": "2016年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on living in the virtual world. Try to imagine what will happen when people spend more and more time in the virtual world instead of interacting in the real world. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "As digital technologies advance at an astonishing pace, more and more people are choosing to live in the\nvirtual world rather than interact face to face. Where will this trend lead us? In my view, spending excessive\ntime online is likely to reshape our lives in worrying ways.\nFirst, human relationships will probably grow more distant. Picture a family sitting in one room at dinner, yet\neach member is silently staring at a glowing screen, connected to strangers online but disconnected from those\nbeside them. As such scenes multiply, our real social skills may gradually decay, since abilities like reading\nemotions and holding a warm conversation only sharpen through practice. What is more, constant immersion\ncould damage both our mental and physical health, leaving many anxious, lonely and out of shape.\nThis does not mean the virtual world is purely harmful; it can indeed link distant hearts and boost efficiency.\nYet the key lies in balance. The more time we surrender to screens, the more deliberately we should return to\nreality. Only by keeping one foot firmly in the real world can we enjoy technology without being enslaved\nby it."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国的创新正以前所未有的速度蓬勃发展。 为了在科学技术上尽快赶超世界发达国家，中国近年来大幅度增加了研究开发资金。 中国的大学和研究所正在积极开展创新研究，这些研究覆盖了从大数据到生物化学、从新能源到机器人等各类高科技领域。 它们还与各地的科技园合作，使创新成果商业化。 与此同时，无论在产品还是商业模式上，中国企业家也在努力争做创新的先锋，以适应国内外消费市场不断变化和增长的需求。", "sample": "Innovation in China is booming at an unprecedented speed. To catch up with and surpass the developed countries in science and technology as soon as possible, China has substantially increased its research and development funding in recent years. China's universities and research institutes are actively carrying out innovative research, which covers various high-tech fields ranging from big data to biochemistry and from new energy to robotics. They also cooperate with science parks across the country to commercialize their innovation achievements. Meanwhile, whether in products or business models, Chinese entrepreneurs are also striving to be pioneers of innovation, so as to meet the changing and growing demands of consumer markets at home and abroad."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2016-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Pursuing a career is an essential part of adolescent development. \"The adolescent becomes an adult when he  __26__  a real job.\" To cognitive researchers like Piaget, adulthood meant the beginning of an  __27__  .\nPiaget argued that once adolescents enter the world of work, their newly acquired ability to form hypotheses allows them to create representations that are too ideal. The  __28__  of such ideals, without the tempering of the reality of a job or profession, rapidly leads adolescents to become  __29__  of the non-idealistic world and to press for reform in a characteristically adolescent way. Piaget said: \"True adaptation to society comes  __30__  when the adolescent reformer attempts to put his ideas to work.\"\nOf course, youthful idealism is often courageous, and no one likes to give up dreams. Perhaps, taken  __31__  out of context, Piaget's statement seems harsh. What he was  __32__  , however, is the way reality can modify idealistic views. Some people refer to such modification as maturity. Piaget argued that attaining and accepting a vocation is one of the best ways to modify idealized views and to mature.\nthat attaining and accepting a vocation is one of the best ways to modify idealized views and to mature. As careers and vocations become less available during times of  __33__  , adolescents may be especially hard hit. Such difficult economic times may leave many adolescents  __34__  about their roles in society. For this reason, community interventions and government job programs that offer summer and vacation work are not only economically  __35__  but also help to stimulate the adolescent's sense of worth.", "matchIntro": "Can Societies be rich and green?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "\"If our economies are to flourish, if global poverty is to be eliminated and if the well-being of the world's people is to be enhanced—not just in this generation but in succeeding generations—we must make sure we take care of the natural environment and resources on which our economic activity depends.\" That statement comes not, as you might imagine, from a stereotypical tree-hugging, save-the-world greenie(环保主义者), but from Gordon Brown, a politician with a reputation for rigour, thoroughness and above all, caution."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A surprising thing for the man who runs one of the world's most powerful economies to say? Perhaps; though in the run-up to the five-year review of the millennium(千年的) Goals, he is far from alone. The roots of his speech, given in March at the roundtable meeting of environment and energy ministers from the G20 group of nations, stretch back to 1972, and the United Nations Conference on the Human Environment in Stockholm."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\"The protection and improvement of the human environment is a major issue which affects the well-being of peoples and economic development throughout the world,\" read the final declaration from this gathering, the first of a sequence which would lead to the Rio de Janeiro Earth Summit in 1992 and the World Development Summit in Johannesburg three years ago."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hunt through the reports prepared by UN agencies and development groups—many for conferences such as this year's Millennium Goals review—and you will find that the linkage between environmental protection and economic progress is a common thread."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Managing ecosystems sustainably is more profitable than exploiting them, according to the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. But finding hard evidence to support the thesis is not so easy. Thoughts turn first to some sort of global statistic, some indicator which would rate the wealth of nations in both economic and environmental terms and show a relationship between the two."}, {"label": "F", "text": "If such an indicator exists, it is well hidden. And on reflection, this is not surprising; the single word \"environment\" has so many dimensions, and there are so many other factors affecting wealth—such as the oil deposits—that teasing out a simple economy-environment relationship would be almost impossible."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, a vast four-year global study which reported its initial conclusions earlier this year, found reasons to believe that managing ecosystems sustainably—working with nature rather than against it—might be less profitable in the short term, but certainly brings long-term rewards."}, {"label": "H", "text": "And the World Resources Institute (WRI) in its World Resources 2005 report, issued at the end of August, produced several such examples from Africa and Asia; it also demonstrated that environmental degradation affects the poor more than the rich, as poorer people derive a much higher proportion of their income directly from the natural resources around them."}, {"label": "I", "text": "But there are also many examples of growing wealth by trashing the environment, in rich and poor parts of the world alike, whether through unregulated mineral extraction, drastic water use for agriculture, slash-and-burn farming, or fossil-fuel-guzzling(大量消耗) transport. Of course, such growth may not persist in the long term—which is what Mr. Brown and the Stockholm declaration were both attempting to point out. Perhaps the best example of boom growth and bust decline is the Grand Banks fishery. For almost five centuries a very large supply of cod(鳕鱼) provided abundant raw material for an industry which at its peak employed about 40,000 people, sustaining entire communities in Newfoundland. Then, abruptly, the cod population collapsed. There were no longer enough fish in the sea for the stock to maintain itself, let alone an industry. More than a decade later, there was no sign of the ecosystem re-building itself. It had, apparently, been fished out of existence; and the once mighty Newfoundland fleet now gropes about frantically for crab on the sea floor."}, {"label": "J", "text": "There is a view that modern humans are inevitably sowing the seed of a global Grand Banks-style disaster. The idea is that we are taking more out of what you might call the planet's environmental bank balance than it can sustain; we are living beyond our ecological means. One recent study attempted to calculate the extent of this \"ecological overshoot of the human economy\", and found that we are using 1.2 Earth's-worth of environmental goods and services—the implication being that at some point the debt will be called in, and all those services—the things which the planet does for us for free—will grind to a halt."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Whether this is right, and if so where and when the ecological axe will fall, is hard to determine with any precision—which is why governments and financial institutions are only beginning to bring such risks into their economic calculations. It is also the reason why development agencies are not united in their view of environmental issues; while some, like the WRI, maintain that environmental progress needs to go hand-in-hand with economic development, others argue that the priority is to build a thriving economy, and then use the wealth created to tackle environmental degradation."}, {"label": "L", "text": "This view assumes that rich societies will invest in environmental care. But is this right? Do things get better or worse as we get richer? Here the Stockholm declaration is ambiguous. \"In the developing countries,\" it says, \"most of the environmental problems are caused by under-development,\" So it is saying that economic development should make for a cleaner world? Not necessarily. \"In the industrialised countries, environmental problems are generally related to industrialisation and technological development,\" it continues. In other words, poor and rich both over-exploit the natural world, but for different reasons. It's simply not true that economic growth will surely make our world cleaner."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Clearly, richer societies are able to provide environmental improvements which lie well beyond the reach of poorer communities. Citizens of wealthy nations demand national parks, clean rivers, clean air and poison-free food. They also, however, use far more natural resources—fuel, water (all those baths and golf courses) and building materials."}, {"label": "N", "text": "A case can be made that rich nations export environmental problems, the most graphic example being climate change. As a country's wealth grows, so do its greenhouse gas emissions. The figures available will not be completely accurate. Measuring emissions is not a precise science, particularly when it comes to issues surrounding land use; not all nations have released up-to-date data, and in any case, emissions from some sectors such as aviation are not included in national statistics. But the data is exact enough for a clear trend to be easily discernible. As countries become richer, they produce more greenhouse gases; and the impact of those gases will fall primarily in poor parts of the world."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Wealth is not, of course, the only factor involved. The average Norwegian is better off than the average US citizen, but contributes about half as much to climate change. But could Norway keep its standard of living and yet cut its emissions to Moroccan or even Ethiopian levels? That question, repeated across a dozen environmental issues and across our diverse planet, is what will ultimately determine whether the human race is living beyond its ecological means as it pursues economic revival."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click on advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater like Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a digital-media consultancy.\nSo the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive advertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism. During commercials, an overlay will appear at the bottom of the screen, prompting viewers to press a button to request a free sample or order a catalogue. Cablevision hopes to allow customers to buy things with their remote controls early next year.\nTelevision advertising could do with a boost. Spending fell by 10% in the first half of the year. The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped. Some are turning to the Internet, which is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates—especially important at a time when marketing budgets are tight. With the launch of interactive advertising, \"many of the dollars that went to the Internet will come back to the TV,\" says David Kline of Cablevision. Or so the industry hopes.\nIn theory, interactive advertising can engage viewers in a way that 30-second spots do not. Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\nThe amount spent on interactive advertising on television is still small. Magna, an advertising agency, reckons it will be worth about $138 million this year. That falls far short of the billions of dollars people once expected it to generate. But DirecTV, Comcast and Time Warner Cable have all invested in it. A new effort led by Canoe Ventures, a coalition of leading cable providers, aims to make interactive advertising available across America later this year. BrightLine ITV, which designs and sells interactive ads, says interest has surged: it expects its revenues almost to triple this year. BSkyB, Britain's biggest satellite-television service, already provides 9 million customers with interactive ads.\nYet there are doubts whether people watching television, a \"lean back\" medium, crave interaction. Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3% online), but that may be a result of the novelty. Interactive ads and viewers might not go well together.", "47": "Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click on advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater like Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a digital-media consultancy.\nSo the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive advertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism. During commercials, an overlay will appear at the bottom of the screen, prompting viewers to press a button to request a free sample or order a catalogue. Cablevision hopes to allow customers to buy things with their remote controls early next year.\nTelevision advertising could do with a boost. Spending fell by 10% in the first half of the year. The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped. Some are turning to the Internet, which is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates—especially important at a time when marketing budgets are tight. With the launch of interactive advertising, \"many of the dollars that went to the Internet will come back to the TV,\" says David Kline of Cablevision. Or so the industry hopes.\nIn theory, interactive advertising can engage viewers in a way that 30-second spots do not. Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\nThe amount spent on interactive advertising on television is still small. Magna, an advertising agency, reckons it will be worth about $138 million this year. That falls far short of the billions of dollars people once expected it to generate. But DirecTV, Comcast and Time Warner Cable have all invested in it. A new effort led by Canoe Ventures, a coalition of leading cable providers, aims to make interactive advertising available across America later this year. BrightLine ITV, which designs and sells interactive ads, says interest has surged: it expects its revenues almost to triple this year. BSkyB, Britain's biggest satellite-television service, already provides 9 million customers with interactive ads.\nYet there are doubts whether people watching television, a \"lean back\" medium, crave interaction. Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3% online), but that may be a result of the novelty. Interactive ads and viewers might not go well together.", "48": "Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click on advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater like Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a digital-media consultancy.\nSo the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive advertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism. During commercials, an overlay will appear at the bottom of the screen, prompting viewers to press a button to request a free sample or order a catalogue. Cablevision hopes to allow customers to buy things with their remote controls early next year.\nTelevision advertising could do with a boost. Spending fell by 10% in the first half of the year. The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped. Some are turning to the Internet, which is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates—especially important at a time when marketing budgets are tight. With the launch of interactive advertising, \"many of the dollars that went to the Internet will come back to the TV,\" says David Kline of Cablevision. Or so the industry hopes.\nIn theory, interactive advertising can engage viewers in a way that 30-second spots do not. Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\nThe amount spent on interactive advertising on television is still small. Magna, an advertising agency, reckons it will be worth about $138 million this year. That falls far short of the billions of dollars people once expected it to generate. But DirecTV, Comcast and Time Warner Cable have all invested in it. A new effort led by Canoe Ventures, a coalition of leading cable providers, aims to make interactive advertising available across America later this year. BrightLine ITV, which designs and sells interactive ads, says interest has surged: it expects its revenues almost to triple this year. BSkyB, Britain's biggest satellite-television service, already provides 9 million customers with interactive ads.\nYet there are doubts whether people watching television, a \"lean back\" medium, crave interaction. Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3% online), but that may be a result of the novelty. Interactive ads and viewers might not go well together.", "49": "Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click on advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater like Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a digital-media consultancy.\nSo the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive advertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism. During commercials, an overlay will appear at the bottom of the screen, prompting viewers to press a button to request a free sample or order a catalogue. Cablevision hopes to allow customers to buy things with their remote controls early next year.\nTelevision advertising could do with a boost. Spending fell by 10% in the first half of the year. The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped. Some are turning to the Internet, which is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates—especially important at a time when marketing budgets are tight. With the launch of interactive advertising, \"many of the dollars that went to the Internet will come back to the TV,\" says David Kline of Cablevision. Or so the industry hopes.\nIn theory, interactive advertising can engage viewers in a way that 30-second spots do not. Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\nThe amount spent on interactive advertising on television is still small. Magna, an advertising agency, reckons it will be worth about $138 million this year. That falls far short of the billions of dollars people once expected it to generate. But DirecTV, Comcast and Time Warner Cable have all invested in it. A new effort led by Canoe Ventures, a coalition of leading cable providers, aims to make interactive advertising available across America later this year. BrightLine ITV, which designs and sells interactive ads, says interest has surged: it expects its revenues almost to triple this year. BSkyB, Britain's biggest satellite-television service, already provides 9 million customers with interactive ads.\nYet there are doubts whether people watching television, a \"lean back\" medium, crave interaction. Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3% online), but that may be a result of the novelty. Interactive ads and viewers might not go well together.", "50": "Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click on advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater like Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a digital-media consultancy.\nSo the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive advertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism. During commercials, an overlay will appear at the bottom of the screen, prompting viewers to press a button to request a free sample or order a catalogue. Cablevision hopes to allow customers to buy things with their remote controls early next year.\nTelevision advertising could do with a boost. Spending fell by 10% in the first half of the year. The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped. Some are turning to the Internet, which is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates—especially important at a time when marketing budgets are tight. With the launch of interactive advertising, \"many of the dollars that went to the Internet will come back to the TV,\" says David Kline of Cablevision. Or so the industry hopes.\nIn theory, interactive advertising can engage viewers in a way that 30-second spots do not. Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\nThe amount spent on interactive advertising on television is still small. Magna, an advertising agency, reckons it will be worth about $138 million this year. That falls far short of the billions of dollars people once expected it to generate. But DirecTV, Comcast and Time Warner Cable have all invested in it. A new effort led by Canoe Ventures, a coalition of leading cable providers, aims to make interactive advertising available across America later this year. BrightLine ITV, which designs and sells interactive ads, says interest has surged: it expects its revenues almost to triple this year. BSkyB, Britain's biggest satellite-television service, already provides 9 million customers with interactive ads.\nYet there are doubts whether people watching television, a \"lean back\" medium, crave interaction. Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3% online), but that may be a result of the novelty. Interactive ads and viewers might not go well together.", "51": "What can be done about mass unemployment? All the wise heads agree: there're no quick or easy answers. There's work to be done, but workers aren't ready to do it—they're in the wrong places, or they have the wrong skills. \"Our problems are structural,\" and will take many years to solve.\nBut don't bother asking for evidence that justifies this bleak view. There isn't any. On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of inadequate demand. Saying that there're no easy answers sounds wise, but it's actually foolish: our unemployment crisis could be cured very quickly if we had the intellectual clarity and political will to act. In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse for not pursuing real solutions.\nThe fact is job openings have plunged in every major sector, while the number of workers forced into part-time employment in almost all industries has soared. Unemployment has surged in every major occupational category. Only three states, with a combined population not much larger than that of Brooklyn, have unemployment rates below 5%. So the evidence contradicts the claim that we're mainly suffering from structural unemployment. Why, then, has this claim become so popular?\nPart of the answer is that this is what always happens during periods of high unemployment—in part because experts and analysts believe that declaring the problem deeply rooted, with no easy answers, makes them sound serious.\nI've been looking at what self-proclaimed experts were saying about unemployment during the Great Depression; it was almost identical to what Very Serious People are saying now. Unemployment cannot be brought down rapidly, declared one 1935 analysis, because the workforce is \"unadaptable and untrained. It cannot respond to the opportunities which industry may offer.\" A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the economy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and untrained\" workers.\nBut now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims that we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do nothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\nSo what you need to know is that there's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims. We aren't suffering from a shortage of needed skills; we're suffering from a lack of policy resolve. As I said, structural unemployment isn't a real problem, it's an excuse—a reason not to act on America's problems at a time when action is desperately needed.", "52": "What can be done about mass unemployment? All the wise heads agree: there're no quick or easy answers. There's work to be done, but workers aren't ready to do it—they're in the wrong places, or they have the wrong skills. \"Our problems are structural,\" and will take many years to solve.\nBut don't bother asking for evidence that justifies this bleak view. There isn't any. On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of inadequate demand. Saying that there're no easy answers sounds wise, but it's actually foolish: our unemployment crisis could be cured very quickly if we had the intellectual clarity and political will to act. In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse for not pursuing real solutions.\nThe fact is job openings have plunged in every major sector, while the number of workers forced into part-time employment in almost all industries has soared. Unemployment has surged in every major occupational category. Only three states, with a combined population not much larger than that of Brooklyn, have unemployment rates below 5%. So the evidence contradicts the claim that we're mainly suffering from structural unemployment. Why, then, has this claim become so popular?\nPart of the answer is that this is what always happens during periods of high unemployment—in part because experts and analysts believe that declaring the problem deeply rooted, with no easy answers, makes them sound serious.\nI've been looking at what self-proclaimed experts were saying about unemployment during the Great Depression; it was almost identical to what Very Serious People are saying now. Unemployment cannot be brought down rapidly, declared one 1935 analysis, because the workforce is \"unadaptable and untrained. It cannot respond to the opportunities which industry may offer.\" A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the economy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and untrained\" workers.\nBut now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims that we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do nothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\nSo what you need to know is that there's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims. We aren't suffering from a shortage of needed skills; we're suffering from a lack of policy resolve. As I said, structural unemployment isn't a real problem, it's an excuse—a reason not to act on America's problems at a time when action is desperately needed.", "53": "What can be done about mass unemployment? All the wise heads agree: there're no quick or easy answers. There's work to be done, but workers aren't ready to do it—they're in the wrong places, or they have the wrong skills. \"Our problems are structural,\" and will take many years to solve.\nBut don't bother asking for evidence that justifies this bleak view. There isn't any. On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of inadequate demand. Saying that there're no easy answers sounds wise, but it's actually foolish: our unemployment crisis could be cured very quickly if we had the intellectual clarity and political will to act. In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse for not pursuing real solutions.\nThe fact is job openings have plunged in every major sector, while the number of workers forced into part-time employment in almost all industries has soared. Unemployment has surged in every major occupational category. Only three states, with a combined population not much larger than that of Brooklyn, have unemployment rates below 5%. So the evidence contradicts the claim that we're mainly suffering from structural unemployment. Why, then, has this claim become so popular?\nPart of the answer is that this is what always happens during periods of high unemployment—in part because experts and analysts believe that declaring the problem deeply rooted, with no easy answers, makes them sound serious.\nI've been looking at what self-proclaimed experts were saying about unemployment during the Great Depression; it was almost identical to what Very Serious People are saying now. Unemployment cannot be brought down rapidly, declared one 1935 analysis, because the workforce is \"unadaptable and untrained. It cannot respond to the opportunities which industry may offer.\" A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the economy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and untrained\" workers.\nBut now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims that we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do nothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\nSo what you need to know is that there's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims. We aren't suffering from a shortage of needed skills; we're suffering from a lack of policy resolve. As I said, structural unemployment isn't a real problem, it's an excuse—a reason not to act on America's problems at a time when action is desperately needed.", "54": "What can be done about mass unemployment? All the wise heads agree: there're no quick or easy answers. There's work to be done, but workers aren't ready to do it—they're in the wrong places, or they have the wrong skills. \"Our problems are structural,\" and will take many years to solve.\nBut don't bother asking for evidence that justifies this bleak view. There isn't any. On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of inadequate demand. Saying that there're no easy answers sounds wise, but it's actually foolish: our unemployment crisis could be cured very quickly if we had the intellectual clarity and political will to act. In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse for not pursuing real solutions.\nThe fact is job openings have plunged in every major sector, while the number of workers forced into part-time employment in almost all industries has soared. Unemployment has surged in every major occupational category. Only three states, with a combined population not much larger than that of Brooklyn, have unemployment rates below 5%. So the evidence contradicts the claim that we're mainly suffering from structural unemployment. Why, then, has this claim become so popular?\nPart of the answer is that this is what always happens during periods of high unemployment—in part because experts and analysts believe that declaring the problem deeply rooted, with no easy answers, makes them sound serious.\nI've been looking at what self-proclaimed experts were saying about unemployment during the Great Depression; it was almost identical to what Very Serious People are saying now. Unemployment cannot be brought down rapidly, declared one 1935 analysis, because the workforce is \"unadaptable and untrained. It cannot respond to the opportunities which industry may offer.\" A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the economy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and untrained\" workers.\nBut now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims that we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do nothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\nSo what you need to know is that there's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims. We aren't suffering from a shortage of needed skills; we're suffering from a lack of policy resolve. As I said, structural unemployment isn't a real problem, it's an excuse—a reason not to act on America's problems at a time when action is desperately needed.", "55": "What can be done about mass unemployment? All the wise heads agree: there're no quick or easy answers. There's work to be done, but workers aren't ready to do it—they're in the wrong places, or they have the wrong skills. \"Our problems are structural,\" and will take many years to solve.\nBut don't bother asking for evidence that justifies this bleak view. There isn't any. On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of inadequate demand. Saying that there're no easy answers sounds wise, but it's actually foolish: our unemployment crisis could be cured very quickly if we had the intellectual clarity and political will to act. In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse for not pursuing real solutions.\nThe fact is job openings have plunged in every major sector, while the number of workers forced into part-time employment in almost all industries has soared. Unemployment has surged in every major occupational category. Only three states, with a combined population not much larger than that of Brooklyn, have unemployment rates below 5%. So the evidence contradicts the claim that we're mainly suffering from structural unemployment. Why, then, has this claim become so popular?\nPart of the answer is that this is what always happens during periods of high unemployment—in part because experts and analysts believe that declaring the problem deeply rooted, with no easy answers, makes them sound serious.\nI've been looking at what self-proclaimed experts were saying about unemployment during the Great Depression; it was almost identical to what Very Serious People are saying now. Unemployment cannot be brought down rapidly, declared one 1935 analysis, because the workforce is \"unadaptable and untrained. It cannot respond to the opportunities which industry may offer.\" A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the economy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and untrained\" workers.\nBut now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims that we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do nothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\nSo what you need to know is that there's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims. We aren't suffering from a shortage of needed skills; we're suffering from a lack of policy resolve. As I said, structural unemployment isn't a real problem, it's an excuse—a reason not to act on America's problems at a time when action is desperately needed."}, "questions": [{"id": "2016-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What position does the woman hold in the company?", "options": ["A) Project organizer.", "B) Public relations officer.", "C) Marketing manager.", "D) Market research consultant."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Market research consultant.\n【定位】M: So, how long have you been a market research consultant?\n男：那么，你做市场调研顾问多久了？\n【信号】提问者把身份直接说破。答案不在女方应答里，而藏在男方开场提问 how long have you\nbeen a market research consultant 里——他张口就点明了对方的职位。对话题的答案句\n不一定是「回答」，提问方代为说出身份时，那句就是答案句。\n【替换】market research consultant ↔ Market research consultant.\n原词原义照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nProject organizer. 录音里只在流程描述时出现过 organize a presentation 的「组织」动\n作，听到 organize 就联想成「项目组织者」是被动词牵着走，原文并无此头衔。\n借用原文：I have to check them and organize a presentation\nB) 未提及\nPublic relations officer. 录音通篇没有出现公关相关说法，纯属凭「市场」联想到「公关」\n的想当然。\nC) 偷换概念\nMarketing manager. 与原文 market research consultant 仅一字之差（marketing\nmanager vs market research consultant），最易混。但原文白纸黑字是 consultant，不\n是 manager，属近义头衔偷换。\n借用原文：market research consultant", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman specialize in at the moment?", "options": ["A) Quantitative advertising research.", "B) Questionnaire design.", "C) Research methodology.", "D) Interviewer training."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Quantitative advertising research.\n【定位】W: Well, at the moment, I specialize in quantitative advertising research, which means that I\ndo two types of projects.\n女：嗯，目前我专攻定量广告调研，也就是说我做两类项目。\n【信号】专长信号词 specialize in。at the moment, I specialize in quantitative advertising\nresearch 里 specialize in 是明晃晃的专长信号，它后面紧跟的方向词就是答案句核心。题\n干的 at the moment 也与此句原词照应，定位极稳。\n【替换】quantitative advertising research ↔ Quantitative advertising research.\n原短语整体照搬\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nQuestionnaire design. 录音确有 I then design a questionnaire，但那是「与新客户合作\n流程」里的一个步骤，不是她「专攻」的方向。听到 questionnaire / design 就选，正中陷\n阱。\n借用原文：I then design a questionnaire\nC) 原词陷阱\nResearch methodology. 源自 we decide on the methodology，也是合作流程的一环，\n非她的专长方向，属典型听到 methodology 就选。\n借用原文：we decide on the methodology\nD) 原词陷阱\nInterviewer training. 录音 Once the interviewers have been briefed 提到\ninterviewers，但说的是「访员被简要交代任务」，并非她的专长，也非 training。\n借用原文：Once the interviewers have been briefed", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say about trackers?", "options": ["A) They are intensive studies of people's spending habits.", "B) They examine relations between producers and customers.", "C) They look for new and effective ways to promote products.", "D) They study trends or customer satisfaction over a long period."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They study trends or customer satisfaction over a long period.\n【定位】Trackers, which are ongoing projects that look at trends or customer satisfaction over a long\nperiod of time.\n追踪式项目，是持续进行的项目，长期考察趋势或顾客满意度。\n【信号】定义信号 which are。Trackers, which are ongoing projects that look at trends or\ncustomer satisfaction over a long period of time 用 which are 直接给 trackers 下定\n义，定义从句就是答案句。抓 ongoing、over a long period 这些定性词。\n【替换】ongoing projects ↔ over a long period.\n持续=长期\nlook at trends or customer satisfaction over a long period of time ↔\nThey study trends or customer satisfaction over a long period.\nlook at=study，且 long period 原词照应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are intensive studies of people's spending habits. 里 intensive（密集/短期高强\n度）恰与原文 over a long period of time（长期）唱反调；spending habits（消费习\n惯）原文也未就 trackers 这么说。这是把「长期」反说成「密集」的对立陷阱，正是预读时\n预警的那一处。\n借用原文：over a long period of time\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey examine relations between producers and customers. 录音 you do build up a\ngood relationship with the client 确有 relationship，但那是说「她与客户建立好关\n系」，被偷换成「研究生产者与顾客的关系」，主体和含义全错。\n借用原文：you do build up a good relationship with the client\nC) 未提及\nThey look for new and effective ways to promote products. 「寻找推广产品的新办\n法」录音中关于 trackers 的描述里完全没有，属凭「市场调研≈促销」的想当然。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman dislike about her job?", "options": ["A) The lack of promotion opportunity.", "B) Checking charts and tables.", "C) Designing questionnaires.", "D) The persistent intensity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Checking charts and tables.\n【定位】W: As I said, variety is important and as for what I don't like, it has to be the checking of\ncharts and tables.\n女：像我说过的，多样性很重要；至于我不喜欢的，那一定是核对图表。\n【信号】好恶信号 what I don't like。as for what I don't like, it has to be the checking of\ncharts and tables 里 what I don't like 是明确的「讨厌」信号，其后 it has to be 引出的\n那件事就是答案。这句还在全对话最末，是压轴好恶。\n【替换】the checking of charts and tables ↔ Checking charts and tables.\n名词化↔动词化，同义照应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe lack of promotion opportunity. 「缺乏晋升机会」录音从未提及，靠「上班族总抱怨\n升不上去」的常识填进来。\nC) 原词陷阱\nDesigning questionnaires. 录音 I then design a questionnaire 确有，但那是中性的流程\n描述，并非她「讨厌」的事——她明确讨厌的是核对图表。听到 design questionnaire 就\n选，落入原词陷阱。\n借用原文：I then design a questionnaire\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe persistent intensity. 暗指原文 it takes up a lot of your time（trackers 很费时）那\n层「持续费力」，但那是 trackers 的「缺点」，不是她整体「讨厌」工作的那一点；她讨厌\n的明确是核对图表，故移花接木。\n借用原文：it takes up a lot of your time", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman want Frederick to talk about?", "options": ["A) His view on Canadian universities.", "B) His understanding of higher education.", "C) His suggestions for improvements in higher education.", "D) His complaint about bureaucracy in American universities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） His view on Canadian universities.\n【定位】OK, and you have very strong views about universities in Canada. Could you please explain?\n好的，你对加拿大的大学有很强烈的看法。能请你解释一下吗？\n【信号】女方的发问句即定位点。这题问\"女方想让他谈什么\"，答案就在女方提问那一句 Could you\nplease explain? 里——她紧接着点了 very strong views about universities in Canada，\n这就是她请他展开的对象。\n【替换】very strong views about universities in Canada ↔ His view on Canadian universities.\nviews=view、in Canada=Canadian\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nB 的 His understanding of higher education. 把话题扩成笼统的\"高等教育\"，但女方明确\n限定在 universities in Canada（加拿大的大学），范围被偷偷放大，答非所问。\nC) 未提及\nC 的 His suggestions for improvements in higher education. 说成\"改进建议\"，但女方\n只请他 explain 看法，没说让他提改进方案，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 His complaint about bureaucracy in American universities. 里 bureaucracy、\nAmerican 确实在后文出现过（女方谈美国时提到），但那不是女方开头请他谈的对象，对\n象被偷换成\"美国\"且时间也错位，属张冠李戴的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：we have many private universities, and I think they're large bureaucracies", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about the curriculum in Canadian universities?", "options": ["A) It is well designed.", "B) It is rather inflexible.", "C) It varies among universities.", "D) It has undergone great changes."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is rather inflexible.\n【定位】All the universities are owned by the government, so there is the Ministry of Education\nin charge of creating the curriculum for the universities and so there's not much room\nfor flexibility.\n所有大学都归政府所有，所以是教育部负责制定大学的课程，因此没有太多灵活的余地。\n【信号】curriculum 后紧跟评价句。答案信号是\"提到 curriculum 后紧接着给的判断\"——男方说\n教育部制定课程，随即下结论 there's not much room for flexibility（没什么灵活空\n间），这句判断就是答案落点。\n【替换】there's not much room for flexibility ↔ It is rather inflexible.\nno flexibility=inflexible 否定转形容词\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 的 It is well designed.（设计得好）是正面评价，而男方整体否定加拿大课程体系（not\nmuch room for flexibility 还说 I don't think it's very efficient），褒贬正相反。\n借用原文：there's not much room for flexibility\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 It varies among universities. 借了原文反复出现的 universities，编出\"各校之间有差\n异\"，但原文说课程是教育部统一制定（Ministry of Education in charge of creating the\ncurriculum），恰恰是统一而非各异，答非所问。\n借用原文：Ministry of Education in charge of creating the curriculum for the universities\nD) 未提及\nD 的 It has undergone great changes.（经历了巨大变化）原文从未提及，男方只谈课程\n死板、低效，没说课程变化大，属凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "On what point do the speakers agree?", "options": ["A) The United States and Canada can learn from each other.", "B) Public universities are often superior to private universities.", "C) Everyone should be given equal access to higher education.", "D) Private schools work more efficiently than public institutions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Everyone should be given equal access to higher education.\n【定位】I agree with you. I think it's a problem because you're not giving equal access to education\nto everybody.\n我同意你的看法。我认为这是个问题，因为你没有给每个人平等接受教育的机会。\n【信号】I agree 后即共识内容。答案信号是男方的认同标志 I agree with you——它后面紧跟\n的 you're not giving equal access to education to everybody 就是两人共同认可的那\n一点。\n【替换】you're not giving equal access to education to everybody ↔\nEveryone should be given equal access to higher education.\n否定现状=应然主张 肯否转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 The United States and Canada can learn from each other. 借了男方 we can learn\nfrom Japan 的 learn from，但原文是\"向日本学习\"而非\"美加互相学习\"，且这是男方单方\n的设想、不是双方明确共同认同的那一点，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Perhaps we can learn from Japan\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 Public universities are often superior to private universities. 借了原文 public\nuniversities are considered to be the best，但那句仅限\"在日本\"，并非两人普遍同意的\n共识；且女方明说美国相反（It's the exact opposite in the United States.），故\"公立普\n遍优于私立\"不是共识。\n借用原文：in Japan, public universities are considered to be the best\nD) 与原文相反\nD 的 Private schools work more efficiently than public institutions. 原文没有任何人这\n么说；女方反而认为私立也是大官僚机构（they're large bureaucracies also）、不见得更\n有动力，与 D 不符。\n借用原文：they're large bureaucracies also", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What point does the man make at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) University systems vary from country to country.", "B) Efficiency is essential to university management.", "C) It is hard to say which is better, a public university or a private one.", "D) Many private universities in the U.S. are actually large bureaucracies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is hard to say which is better, a public university or a private one.\n【定位】So, as you see, it's very hard to say which one is better.\n所以，如你所见，很难说哪一个更好。\n【信号】So, as you see 收束信号。答案信号是结尾的收束词 So, as you see——它之后那句 it's\nvery hard to say which one is better 是男方对全场讨论的总结落点。\n【替换】it's very hard to say which one is better ↔\nIt is hard to say which is better, a public university or a private one.\nwhich one=which 公立私立 指代补全\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 的 University systems vary from country to country. 听上去呼应了对话涉及的加、\n美、日多国，但男方结尾说的是\"难说哪个更好\"，不是\"各国制度不同\"，把结论换了方向。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 Efficiency is essential to university management. 借了原文多次出现的\nefficient/efficiency，但男方谈效率是在评价加拿大体制（I don't think it's very\nefficient），并非结尾的总结观点，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：I don't think it's very efficient\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 的 Many private universities in the U.S. are actually large bureaucracies. 几乎照搬了\n原文，但说这话的是女方（in the United States we have many private universities, and\nI think they're large bureaucracies），本题问的是男方结尾观点，说话人对错了。\n借用原文：we have many private universities, and I think they're large bureaucracies also", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the International Labor Organization's report mainly about?", "options": ["A) Government's role in resolving an economic crisis.", "B) The worsening real wage situation around the world.", "C) Indications of economic recovery in the United States.", "D) The impact of the current economic crisis on people's life."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） The worsening real wage situation around the world.\n【定位】A recent International Labor Organization report says the deterioration of real wages around\nthe world calls into question the true extent of an economic recovery. The report warns the\npicture on wages is likely to get worse this year, despite indications of an economic rebound.\n国际劳工组织最近一份报告称，全球实际工资的恶化使人们对经济复苏的真实程度产生质\n疑。该报告警告说，尽管有经济回升的迹象，今年的工资状况仍可能进一步恶化。\n【信号】开头点题 + 让步转折后的落点。答案信号有两处叠加：一是 A recent International Labor\nOrganization report says 开篇即点出报告主题；二是让步转折结构 the picture on\nwages is likely to get worse this year, despite indications of an economic rebound\n——despite（尽管）这个让步词把重心压在\"工资会更糟\"上，而非\"经济回升\"上。\n【替换】the deterioration of real wages around the world ↔\nThe worsening real wage situation around the world\ndeterioration 换成 worsening，同义\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n选项 Government's role in resolving an economic crisis. 把焦点放在\"政府的角色\"。原文\n确实提到政府 policies 和 the government must provide wage subsidies，但那只是第\n三、四段举的一个应对例子，不是全篇主旨；报告通篇讲的是\"工资恶化\"。这是用\"经济危\n机\"的氛围词诱你误选。\n借用原文：the government must provide wage subsidies\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Indications of economic recovery in the United States. 里的 the United States 原\n文 They include the United States, Austria, Costa Rica, South Africa and Germany 确\n实念到了，但它只是\"工资停滞/下降\"国家清单里的一员，且选项说的是 economic\nrecovery（经济复苏），方向恰与全文\"工资恶化\"相反。听到 United States 就选正中圈\n套。\n借用原文：They include the United States, Austria, Costa Rica, South Africa and Germany\nD) 未提及\n选项 The impact of the current economic crisis on people's life. 借了 economic crisis\n这个原文氛围词，但 impact on people's life（对人们生活的影响）全文从未展开——报告\n只讲工资数据与应对方案，没讲对个人生活的具体影响。属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "According to an International Labor Organization's specialist, how will employers feel if there are more people looking for jobs?", "options": ["A) They will feel less pressure to raise employees' wages.", "B) They will feel free to choose the most suitable employees.", "C) They will feel inclined to expand their business operations.", "D) They will feel more confident in competing with their rivals."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They will feel less pressure to raise employees' wages.\n【定位】more people will be unemployed, more people will be looking for jobs and the pressure on\nemployers to raise wages to attract workers will decline.\n会有更多人失业、更多人找工作，于是雇主为吸引劳动力而涨薪的压力将会下降。\n【信号】条件路标 looking for jobs 后紧跟因果落点 decline。题干路标 more people looking\nfor jobs 在原文以 more people will be looking for jobs 原样出现，紧随其后的 and 引出\n结果 the pressure on employers to raise wages to attract workers will decline——\ndecline（下降）就是雇主的反应所在，是这句的答案落点。\n【替换】the pressure on employers to raise wages to attract workers will decline ↔\nThey will feel less pressure to raise employees' wages\npressure…will decline 等于 feel less pressure\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n选项 They will feel free to choose the most suitable employees. 看似顺理成章——人\n多了当然挑得动——但原文只说雇主\"涨薪压力下降\"the pressure…will decline，从没说雇\n主\"可以自由挑选最合适的人\"。这是从原文真说过的\"求职者增多\"硬推出的额外结论。\n借用原文：more people will be looking for jobs\nC) 未提及\n选项 They will feel inclined to expand their business operations. 讲\"想扩张业务\"，原\n文围绕的是\"工资压力\"，从未提及雇主因求职者增多就要扩张经营。属无中生有。\nD) 未提及\n选项 They will feel more confident in competing with their rivals. 谈\"和对手竞争更有\n信心\"，原文谈的是雇主对\"涨薪\"的压力，与\"竞争对手\"毫无关系。属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the speaker mean by the Work Sharing scheme?", "options": ["A) Employees and companies cooperate to pull through the economic crisis.", "B) Government and companies join hands to create jobs for the unemployed.", "C) Employees work shorter hours to avoid layoffs.", "D) Team work will be encouraged in companies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Employees work shorter hours to avoid layoffs.\n【定位】An example of these is work sharing with government subsidies. Under this scheme, the\nnumber of individual working hours is reduced in an effort to avoid layoffs.\n其中一个例子是配有政府补贴的\"工作分担\"。在这一方案下，个人工作时数被削减，以努力\n避免裁员。\n【信号】方案名后紧跟 Under this scheme 的解释句。听到方案名 work sharing 后，紧接的\nUnder this scheme, the number of individual working hours is reduced in an effort\nto avoid layoffs 就是对它的正式解释——Under this scheme（在此方案下）是典型的释\n义引导，后面的内容即答案。\n【替换】the number of individual working hours is reduced ↔ Employees work shorter hours\n工时被削减 = 员工上更短的班\nin an effort to avoid layoffs ↔ to avoid layoffs\navoid layoffs 原词对应，目的一致\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 Employees and companies cooperate to pull through the economic crisis. 方向\n上不算错（整篇确在讲应对危机），但它只说\"员工与公司合作渡过危机\"，完全没提 Work\nSharing 的具体做法（缩工时、避裁员）。问\"方案指什么\"，要的是机制，不是空泛的好\n话。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Government and companies join hands to create jobs for the unemployed. 借了\n原文真有的 government（government subsidies）和 jobs 概念，但方案核心是 avoid\nlayoffs（保住现有岗位），选项却说 create jobs（为失业者创造新岗位）——\"保旧岗\"被\n偷换成\"造新岗\"，与原文不符。\n借用原文：work sharing with government subsidies\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Team work will be encouraged in companies. 利用 work sharing 里 sharing（分\n担）一词容易让人联想到\"团队协作\"，但原文的 work sharing 指\"分担工作时数（缩短工\n时）\"，与 team work（团队合作）毫无关系。原文从未提鼓励团队合作。\n借用原文：work sharing with government subsidies", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What question is frequently put to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Whether memory supplements work.", "B) Whether herbal medicine works wonders.", "C) Whether exercise enhances one's memory.", "D) Whether a magic memory promises success."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Whether memory supplements work.\n【定位】I have been frequently asked if these memory supplements work.\n我经常被问到这些记忆补剂到底管不管用。\n【信号】frequently asked原句直陈。录音用 I have been frequently asked if these memory\nsupplements work 直接陈述\"被频繁问到的问题\"，frequently asked 与题干 frequently\nput 对应，是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】asked if these memory supplements work ↔ Whether memory supplements work\nif=whether同义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 a herbal recall remedy，B 借这个 herbal 设饵，但说话人被频繁问的是\nsupplements 是否有用，不是 herbal medicine works wonders（草药是否有神效），盗\n了原词却偷换了问题对象。\n借用原文：a herbal recall remedy\nC) 未提及\nC 说被问的是 Whether exercise enhances one's memory（锻炼能否增强记忆），但开\n头被频繁问的问题里没有 exercise；锻炼是后文 work-out 才引出的另一层意思，不是这题\n问的设问内容。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音开头有 a magic memory pill，D 借 magic 设饵，但说话人被问的是补剂是否有用，\n不是 Whether a magic memory promises success（神奇记忆能否带来成功），\npromises success 是原文没有的夸大延伸。\n借用原文：a magic memory pill", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about most memory supplements?", "options": ["A) They help the elderly more than the young.", "B) They are beneficial in one way or another.", "C) They generally do not have side effects.", "D) They are not based on real science."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They are not based on real science.\n【定位】The science isn't there behind most of them. They're not really well-regulated unless they\nadhere to some industry standard.\n它们大多数背后并没有科学支撑。它们也没有受到很好的监管，除非遵循某种行业标准。\n【信号】否定句science isn't there。录音用否定句 The science isn't there behind most of them\n直接给出评判——大多数补剂背后没有科学，否定语气是态度题的答案信号。\n【替换】The science isn't there behind most of them ↔ They are not based on real science\n同义否定改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说补剂 help the elderly more than the young（对老人比年轻人更有用），录音通篇没\n把人群分成老人/年轻人比较，是凭空安上的对比。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说补剂 beneficial in one way or another（多少有益），方向与录音相反——说话人强\n调 those supplements aren't going to give you that perfect memory 且 the science\nisn't there，是怀疑而非肯定。\n借用原文：those supplements aren't going to give you that perfect memory in the way that\nthey promise\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说补剂 do not have side effects（没有副作用），但录音后文强调 these are\nmedicines, so they do have an impact，明说有影响/风险，C 与原文相反。\n借用原文：these are medicines, so they do have an impact", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about memory supplements in eastern cultures?", "options": ["A) They are available at most country fairs.", "B) They are taken in relatively high dosage.", "C) They are collected or grown by farmers.", "D) They are prescribed by trained practitioners."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are prescribed by trained practitioners.\n【定位】In fact, they are prescribed and they're given at a certain level, a dosage that is understood by\na practitioner who's been trained.\n事实上，它们是由受过训练的医师按其掌握的某一剂量水平开方给出的。\n【信号】eastern定位+prescribed应答。题干限定词 eastern cultures 把定位指向 especially in\nsome eastern cultures 一带，紧接的 they are prescribed… by a practitioner who's\nbeen trained 正面回答该情境下补剂怎么给，是答案句。\n【替换】they are prescribed ↔ They are prescribed\nprescribed原意对应\na practitioner who's been trained ↔ trained practitioners\n语序改写同义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说补剂 available at most country fairs（多在乡村集市可买到），但录音明说 People\ndon't just go in a local grocery store and buy these supplements，即并非随便买到，\n方向相反。\n借用原文：People don't just go in a local grocery store and buy these supplements\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 a dosage that is understood by a practitioner，B 借 dosage 设饵，但原文说\n的是\"医师掌握的某一剂量水平\"，不是 taken in relatively high dosage（服用高剂量），\n盗了原词却换成\"高剂量\"这一原文没有的说法。\n借用原文：a dosage that is understood by a practitioner who's been trained\nC) 未提及\nC 说补剂 collected or grown by farmers（由农民采集种植），录音谈的是开方与剂量，\n从未提及由农民种植采集，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker say about memory supplements at the end?", "options": ["A) They have often proved to be as helpful as doing mental exercise.", "B) Taking them with other medications might entail unnecessary risks.", "C) Their effect lasts only a short time.", "D) Many have benefited from them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Taking them with other medications might entail unnecessary risks.\n【定位】Taking them in combination with other medications might put you at increased risk for\nsomething that you wouldn't otherwise be countering or be at risk for.\n把它们和其他药物一起服用，可能会让你面临某种本来不会遇到、不会有的额外风险。\n【信号】结尾风险句might put at risk。录音结尾用 taking them in combination with other\nmedications might put you at increased risk 给出最后的警示，might put… at\nincreased risk 是结尾结论性风险句，正对 at the end 的提问。\n【替换】taking them in combination with other medications ↔ Taking them with other medications\nin combination=with同义\nmight put you at increased risk ↔ might entail unnecessary risks\n增加风险同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说补剂 as helpful as doing mental exercise（和做脑力锻炼一样有用），录音虽提过\nwork-out/mental exercise 的概念，但从未说补剂\"和锻炼一样有效\"，这个对等比较是原文\n没有的。\nC) 未提及\nC 说 Their effect lasts only a short time（效果只持续很短时间），录音结尾谈的是合用\n风险，从未涉及效果的持续时长，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说 Many have benefited from them（许多人从中获益），与全篇怀疑、结尾警示风险\n的基调相反，说话人结尾强调的是风险而非获益。\n借用原文：might put you at increased risk for something that you wouldn't otherwise be\ncountering or be at risk for", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is the talk mainly about?", "options": ["A) How catastrophic natural disasters turn out to be to developing nations.", "B) How the World Meteorological Organization studies natural disasters.", "C) How powerless humans appear to be in face of natural disasters.", "D) How the negative impacts of natural disasters can be reduced."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） How the negative impacts of natural disasters can be reduced.\n【定位】He says extreme events will continue. But, he says extreme events become disasters only\nwhen people fail to prepare for them.\n他说极端事件还会继续发生。但是，他说极端事件只有在人们没能为之做好准备时才会变成\n灾难。\n【信号】转折But后是真意。全文开头铺陈灾害多可怕，到 But, he says extreme events become\ndisasters only when people fail to prepare for them. 用转折词 But 把调子扭向人若准备\n好就不会成灾，配合第 3 段 they need not be disasters，主线落在损失可以被减少。转折\n后才是讲者真正想说的主旨。\n【替换】extreme events become disasters only when people fail to prepare for them ↔\nthe negative impacts of natural disasters can be reduced\n反面说=正面减损\nthe loss of life has decreased by a factor of 10 simply because we are getting better at warning\npeople\n↔ can be reduced\ndecreased=reduced\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音确实念了 nearly 8,400 natural disasters killed more than two-million people 等灾\n害数据，A 借此暗示灾害对发展中国家多严重；但全文从未把焦点限定在 developing\nnations，举的古巴、孟加拉是减灾成功例，不是发展中国家受害有多惨。属缩小并跑偏的\n范围。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 World Meteorological Organization，B 把它说成全文在讲该组织怎样研究灾\n害；但录音只是借这个机构引出观点，全篇主线是怎样减少损失，不是介绍某机构的研究方\n法。听到机构名就选，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：director of Weather and Disaster Risk Reduction at the World\nMeteorological Organization\nC) 与原文相反\n开头 The negative impacts of natural disasters can be seen everywhere. 确给人人类很\n无力之感，C 顺势说全文讲人类多无力；但这只是 But 转折前的铺垫，讲者随即用 We are\nmaking a difference 推翻它。C 取了转折前被否定的旧基调，与全文真实落点相反。\n借用原文：The negative impacts of natural disasters can be seen everywhere.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "How can we stop extreme events from turning into disasters?", "options": ["A) By training rescue teams for emergencies.", "B) By taking steps to prepare people for them.", "C) By changing people's views of nature.", "D) By relocating people to safer places."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） By taking steps to prepare people for them.\n【定位】But, he says extreme events become disasters only when people fail to prepare for them.\n但是，他说极端事件只有在人们没能为之做好准备时才会变成灾难。\n【信号】only when条件即答案。录音用 extreme events become disasters only when people\nfail to prepare for them 点出成灾的唯一条件是没准备好，反过来说就是做好准备就能阻\n止成灾。only when 这个限定条件句正是讲对策的落点，与第 7 段 taking preventive\naction 互相印证。\n【替换】people fail to prepare for them ↔ taking steps to prepare people for them\n否定前提反推做法\ntaking preventive action ↔ taking steps to prepare people for them\npreventive=prepare\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音讲的对策是 Build better buildings、make sure the warnings go right down to the\ncommunity level 这类事前防范，从没说训练救援队应急。A 的 training rescue teams 是\n录音未提的另一种办法，听起来合理但无据。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音多次提到 nature 相关的灾害，C 顺势说办法是改变人们对自然的看法；但全文给的对\n策是盖好房子、做好预警、提前准备，从没说要改变观念。借话题词 nature 制造的答非所\n问。\n借用原文：caused by weather, climate, or water-related extremes\nD) 偷换概念\n录音 Don't build where the hazards will destroy them. 说的是别在危险地带盖房，D 把\n它歪成把人迁到安全地（relocating people）。原文讲的是建房选址，不是迁移人口，意\n思被偷换。\n借用原文：Don't build where the hazards will destroy them.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the example of Cuba serve to show?", "options": ["A) How preventive action can reduce the loss of life.", "B) How courageous Cubans are in face of disasters.", "C) How Cubans suffer from tropical storms.", "D) How destructive tropical storms can be."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） How preventive action can reduce the loss of life.\n【定位】It says tropical storms formerly claimed dozens, if not hundreds of lives, each year in Cuba.\nBut the development of an early-warning system has reversed that trend. In 2008, Cuba was\nhit by five successive hurricanes, but only seven people were killed.\n据该组织称，热带风暴过去在古巴每年夺去数十乃至上百条生命。但预警系统的建立扭转了\n这一趋势。2008 年古巴接连遭受五场飓风，却只有七人遇难。\n【信号】But后死亡数骤降。古巴段用 But the development of an early-warning system has\nreversed that trend. 的转折点明预警系统扭转了局面，再用 only seven people were\nkilled 这个对比数字证明效果。配合第 7 段 reduced the loss of life caused by natural\ndisasters by taking preventive action，这个例子是为预防能救命这一论点服务。\n【替换】the development of an early-warning system has reversed that trend ↔\npreventive action can reduce the loss of life\n预警=预防行动\nonly seven people were killed ↔ reduce the loss of life\n只死七人=减损\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音只讲古巴靠预警系统把死亡降到很低，从未评价古巴人勇敢。B 的 courageous 是凭灾\n害情境臆想出来的褒义评价，原文毫无依据。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音 tropical storms formerly claimed dozens, if not hundreds of lives 确说古巴过去深\n受风暴之害，C 据此说例子讲古巴人多惨；但这是 But 转折前的旧情况，例子重点是其后被\n扭转的好结果，C 取错了重心。\n借用原文：tropical storms formerly claimed dozens, if not hundreds of lives, each year in Cuba\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 five successive hurricanes 等风暴字眼，D 借此说例子在讲热带风暴多有破坏\n力；但古巴例子恰恰是为证明预防能救命，不是渲染风暴威力。听到 hurricanes/storms 就\n选，落入原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Cuba was hit by five successive hurricanes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does President Obama hope the banks will do?", "options": ["A) Pay back their loans to the American government.", "B) Provide loans to those in severe financial difficulty.", "C) Contribute more to the goal of a wider recovery.", "D) Speed up their recovery from the housing bubble."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Contribute more to the goal of a wider recovery.\n【定位】\"The way I see it is banks now having greater obligation to the goal of a wider recovery,\"\nhe said.\n「在我看来，银行如今对实现更广泛复苏负有更大的责任，」他说。\n【信号】说话人直接表态句。答案落在奥巴马本人的引语 banks now having greater obligation to\nthe goal of a wider recovery——题问『奥巴马希望银行做什么』，答案就在他亲口说出\n的那句表态里。\n【替换】greater obligation to the goal of a wider recovery ↔\nContribute more to the goal of a wider recovery.\n负有更大责任=应多出力\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音开头说 telling them it's time to return the favor（该回报了），『return the\nfavor』听着像『还钱给政府』，但奥巴马随后明确的期望是为更广复苏出力，不是字面还\n贷，A 把模糊的『回报』坐实成『还 loans 给政府』，属偷换。\n借用原文：telling them it's time to return the favor\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音通篇出现 loans、financial、difficulty 这类词（如 credit availability to small and\nmedium sized businesses），B 用 Provide loans to those in severe financial\ndifficulty. 把这些原词拼起来，但奥巴马希望银行做的是为整体复苏出力，并未要求向困难\n者放贷，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：credit availability to small and medium sized businesses\nD) 偷换概念\n录音提到 the collapse of the housing bubble，D 用 Speed up their recovery from the\nhousing bubble. 盗用 housing bubble 一词，且把 recovery 的落点从『更广泛的复苏\n（整体）』偷换成『银行自身从泡沫中恢复』，方向变了。\n借用原文：the collapse of the housing bubble", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is Martin Neil Baily's prediction about the financial situation in the future?", "options": ["A) Some banks may have to merge with others.", "B) Many smaller regional banks are going to fail.", "C) It will be hard for banks to provide more loans.", "D) Many banks will have to lay off some employees."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Many smaller regional banks are going to fail.\n【定位】He predicts high failure rates for smaller, regional banks in 2010 as commercial real estate\nloans come due.\n他预测，随着商业地产贷款到期，2010 年小型地区银行的倒闭率会很高。\n【信号】预测信号词 predicts。答案句由 He predicts 这个明确的预测信号引出——题问 Baily 的\nprediction，听到 predicts 就锁定其后内容。\n【替换】high failure rates for smaller, regional banks ↔ Many smaller regional banks are going to fail.\n高倒闭率=很多银行将倒闭\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只预测小型地区银行倒闭率高，从未提到银行『合并（merge）』。A 的 Some banks\nmay have to merge with others. 是录音未提及的内容。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说过 there may actually be a worsening of credit availability（放贷会更难），\nC 的 It will be hard for banks to provide more loans. 正盗用这句，但那是承接 Baily 倒\n闭预测后的延伸说法，本题问的是他的『预测』核心，正解是倒闭率高；C 偷换成放贷难这\n一旁支。\n借用原文：there may actually be a worsening of credit availability\nD) 未提及\n录音谈倒闭、放贷难、失业（unemployment），但失业指的是整体经济的高失业率，并非\n银行『裁员』。D 的 Many banks will have to lay off some employees. 是录音未提到的\n银行行为。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does U.S. Bancorp chief Richard Davis say about its future operation?", "options": ["A) It will work closely with the government.", "B) It will endeavor to write off bad loans.", "C) It will try to lower the interest rate.", "D) It will try to provide more loans."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It will try to provide more loans.\n【定位】\"...lending is the coal to our engine, so we want to make more loans. We have to find a way to\nqualify more people and not put ourselves at risk.\"\n「……放贷是我们引擎的燃料，所以我们想发放更多贷款。我们得想办法让更多人符合贷款\n资格，同时又不让自己陷入风险。」\n【信号】本人引语+意愿句 we want to。答案落在 Davis 本人引语中的意愿表态 we want to make\nmore loans——题问他对未来经营怎么说，盯住他『we want to…』的直接陈述即可。\n【替换】we want to make more loans ↔ It will try to provide more loans.\n想多放贷=将设法多放贷\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音前文反复出现 government（如 the help of the American government），A 的 It\nwill work closely with the government. 盗用这个高频词，但 Davis 本人引语谈的是多放\n贷、让更多人合格，从没说要与政府密切合作，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the help of the American government\nB) 原词陷阱\nDavis 谈的是想多放贷、防自身风险，从未说要『核销坏账（write off bad loans）』。B\n的 It will endeavor to write off bad loans. 借用 loans 一词，却讲了录音未提的动作。\n借用原文：we want to make more loans\nC) 未提及\nDavis 引语只字未提『降低利率（lower the interest rate）』。C 的 It will try to lower\nthe interest rate. 是录音未出现的内容，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does Martin Neil Baily think of a second stimulus to the economy?", "options": ["A) It won't help the American economy to turn around.", "B) It won't do any good to the major commercial banks.", "C) It will win the approval of the Obama administration.", "D) It will be necessary if the economy starts to shrink again."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It will be necessary if the economy starts to shrink again.\n【定位】If the economy starts to shrink again, Baily says it would make a strong case for a second\nstimulus—something the Obama administration hopes will not be necessary.\nBaily 说，如果经济再度萎缩，那将为第二次刺激提供有力理由——而这正是奥巴马政府希\n望不会成为必要的事。\n【信号】条件句 If…+ Baily says。答案落在 Baily 的条件性判断 If the economy starts to shrink\nagain, Baily says it would make a strong case for a second stimulus——态度题盯住带\nif 的条件句，它讲清了『在什么前提下第二次刺激才有必要』。\n【替换】If the economy starts to shrink again, Baily says it would make a strong case for a second stimulus\n↔ It will be necessary if the economy starts to shrink again.\n提供有力理由=有必要\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nBaily 是说经济再萎缩时第二次刺激有必要（有助经济），A 的 It won't help the\nAmerican economy to turn around. 却给出否定评价，与他的看法极性相反。\n借用原文：it would make a strong case for a second stimulus\nB) 原词陷阱\nBaily 谈的是第二次刺激对整体经济在特定条件下有必要，从没说它对『主要商业银行\n（major commercial banks）』有没有好处。B 的 It won't do any good to the major\ncommercial banks. 借用录音里的 commercial（来自 commercial real estate loans）一\n词，却讲了 Baily 未表达的判断。\n借用原文：commercial real estate loans\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说第二次刺激是 something the Obama administration hopes will not be\nnecessary（奥巴马政府希望它不必要），C 的 It will win the approval of the Obama\nadministration. 盗用 Obama administration，却把『政府希望不需要』说成『将获政府\n认可』，恰好反了，且这本就不是 Baily 的看法。\n借用原文：something the Obama administration hopes will not be necessary", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "According to the speaker, what might be a symptom of cognitive decline in older adults?", "options": ["A) Being unable to learn new things.", "B) Being rather slow to make changes.", "C) Losing temper more and more often.", "D) Losing the ability to get on with others."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Being unable to learn new things.\n【定位】Cognitive decline is the loss of ability to learn new skills, or recall words, names, and faces\nthat is most common as we age.\n认知衰退是指学习新技能，或回忆词语、姓名和面孔的能力丧失，这在我们变老时最为\n常见。\n【信号】下定义句即答案句。录音用 Cognitive decline is the loss of ability to learn new skills\n给认知衰退\"下定义\"，is 这个判断系动词后面就是它的具体表现，定义句正是本题答案句\n所在。\n【替换】loss of ability to learn new skills ↔ Being unable to learn new things\n丧失能力=无法做\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 Being rather slow to make changes 借了录音反复出现的 lifestyle changes 里的\n\"changes\"一词来诱选，但录音说的是\"改变生活方式\"，从没说衰退表现为\"做改变变慢\"，\n属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：lifestyle changes\nC) 未提及\nC 的 Losing temper more and more often（越来越爱发脾气）属情绪维度，录音定义衰\n退只谈\"学习/记忆能力\"，从未提及脾气情绪，是凭日常对\"老人\"的刻板印象想当然的未提及\n项。\nD) 偷换概念\nD 的 Losing the ability to get on with others 借了定义句里 \"loss of ability\"的句式来仿\n冒，但录音 recall words, names, and faces 说的是记忆能力，从没说丧失\"与人相处的能\n力\"，是套用原文句式却偷换内容。\n借用原文：recall words, names, and faces", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "According to James Burke, what does seem to help reduce cognitive decline?", "options": ["A) Cognitive stimulation.", "B) Community activity.", "C) Balanced diet.", "D) Fresh air."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Cognitive stimulation.\n【定位】And Burke says that actually does seem to help, based on randomized studies—the\nresearcher's gold standard.\nBurke 说，基于随机对照研究（研究者的黄金标准），这一点确实似乎有帮助。\n【信号】actually does help 强调有效。第 5 段 that actually does seem to help 与题\n干 \"does seem to help\"几乎同词，且 actually 一词把它从前文\"证据不强\"中拎出来\n重点强调，是答案落点；它指的正是上句的 challenging your brain with mentally\nstimulating activities。\n【替换】challenging your brain with mentally stimulating activities ↔ Cognitive stimulation\n脑力刺激活动=认知刺激\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 的 Community activity 听起来与\"参与活动\"沾边，但录音通篇说的是 being\nintellectually engaged（智力上投入），从没提社区活动，是借\"活动\"概念想当然的未提\n及项。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 Balanced diet 盗用了录音多次出现的 diet，极易\"听到 diet 就选\"；但 Burke 对 diet\n的原话是 the evidence was not so strong that we could actually consider these firmly\nestablished（证据不够强、不能确认），它被归到\"证据不足\"一类，不是\"确实有帮助\"。\n借用原文：the evidence was not so strong that we could actually consider these firmly established\nD) 未提及\nD 的 Fresh air 最迎合\"多呼吸新鲜空气有益\"的日常常识，但录音研究的因素是 smoking,\ndiet, brain-challenging games, exercise 等，从未出现\"新鲜空气\"，是纯常识杜撰的未提\n及项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What did James Burke recommend to reduce the incidence of cognitive decline?", "options": ["A) Ignoring the signs and symptoms of aging.", "B) Adopting an optimistic attitude towards life.", "C) Endeavoring to give up unhealthy lifestyles.", "D) Seeking advice from doctors from time to time."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Endeavoring to give up unhealthy lifestyles.\n【定位】I think that by having people adopt a healthy lifestyle, both from a medical standpoint as\nwell as nutritional and cognitive stimulation standpoint, we can reduce the incidence of\ncognitive decline.\n我认为，通过让人们采取健康的生活方式——既包括医学角度，也包括营养和认知刺激角度\n——我们可以降低认知衰退的发生率。\n【信号】I think+adopt 即建议句。第 8 段 I think that by having people adopt a healthy\nlifestyle 以 \"I think\"领起、用 adopt 给出做法，正是 Burke 发出建议的句子；上一段\nBurke was willing to offer some recommendations 也预告了这里要出建议，答案就锁在\n此句。\n【替换】adopt a healthy lifestyle ↔ Endeavoring to give up unhealthy lifestyles\n采取健康=改掉不健康\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 的 Ignoring the signs and symptoms of aging 借了第 2 段定义衰退\"as we age\"的衰\n老话题，但 Burke 是叫人积极改变生活方式，而非\"无视衰老迹象\"，方向恰好相反。\n借用原文：most common as we age\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 Adopting an optimistic attitude towards life 借了 make positive changes 里\npositive 的\"积极\"味道来诱选，但 positive 在录音里指\"积极的改变/有益效果\"，不是\"乐观\n心态\"；Burke 从没建议保持乐观态度。\n借用原文：make positive changes\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 Seeking advice from doctors from time to time 借了第 8 段 from a medical\nstandpoint 的\"medical\"来制造\"提到医生\"的错觉，但录音说的是\"从医学角度采取健康生\n活方式\"，并没有建议\"常去找医生咨询\"。\n借用原文：from a medical standpoint", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) undertakes\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 he 后缺第三人称单数谓语\n【依据】\"承担一份真正的工作\"贴合\"踏入成年\"的定义\nThe adolescent becomes an adult when he __(26)__ a real job. 给青少年走向成年下定\n义——开始\"承担\"一份真正的工作，undertake a job 是地道搭配，表示主动去做、担起责\n任，正契合\"成年=能担当工作\"的论点。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 he 后缺第三人称单数谓语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) occupation\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 an 后名词位\n【依据】与上句\"real job\"同义复指\n上一句 The adolescent becomes an adult when he __(26)__ a real job. 给出\"真正的工\n作\"，紧接着皮亚杰把\"成年\"定义为 the beginning of an occupation，occupation 与 real\njob 形成同义复指，主题词\"职业\"一锤定音。\n【竞争词】F) entrance \"入口\"与\"开始\"语义冗余\nentrance 本身已含\"进入\"之意，与前面的 the beginning of 在语义上叠床架屋，\"开始一个入\n口\"逻辑别扭。\nH) existence \"生存\"过于宏大，背离\"职业\"焦点\nexistence 指\"生存状态\"，意涵宽泛；本段聚焦点是\"a real job\"，非\"开始一种生存\"——过于宏\n大反而偏题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定冠词 an 后名词位"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) existence\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 The + ___ + of 名词位\n【依据】\"理想的存在/产生\"对应皮亚杰\"形成假设之后才出现理想\"\n上一句 their newly acquired ability to form hypotheses allows them to create\nrepresentations that are too ideal. 说青少年才有了构建过度理想化表征的能力，本句承\n接说\"这些理想的 existence（产生/存在）若没有现实磨砺，就会让他们对非理想世界\nintolerant\"——existence 表达\"理想已经在心里存在/出现\"这一状态，是承接 create\nrepresentations 的最佳抽象名词。\n【竞争词】F) entrance \"入口\"无法搭 of such ideals\nentrance of such ideals 字面成\"这些理想的入口\"，意象不通；entrance 通常指物理或场景的\n进入处，与抽象\"理想\"无法搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定冠词 The + ___ + of 名"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) intolerant\n形容词\n【词性槽】become + ___ + of 形容词位\n【依据】\"理想 vs 现实\"语境配 intolerant of\n前文说青少年理想化、缺少现实磨砺，紧接着 rapidly leads adolescents to become\n__(29)__ of the non-idealistic world and to press for reform in a characteristically\nadolescent way. 描述青少年迫切想改革——这是对\"非理想世界\"无法忍受的典型表现；\nintolerant of 正好搭配 of 介词，且语义对接\"按青少年式做派激烈推动改革\"。\n【竞争词】B) beneficial 义为\"有益的\"，与下文\"press for reform\"语气反向\nbeneficial 是褒义且通常搭 to 而非 of；说\"对非理想世界有益\"既不通搭配，也与紧随其后的\"急\n于改革\"语气拧反。\nD) confused 义为\"困惑的\"，与\"激进改革\"语义不合\n困惑者会犹豫不决，而原文描述的是\"激切推动改革\"——情绪是义愤而非茫然；且 confused 多\n搭 by/about，不搭 of。\nG) excited 义为\"兴奋的\"，搭配为 excited about/at\nexcited 表对某事兴奋好奇，常搭 about/at，不与 of 搭配；语义上对非理想世界\"兴奋\"也讲\n不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·become + ___ + of 形容"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) automatically\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 comes 后缺状语\n【依据】对应\"理想唯有付诸实践才自然而然达成适应\"\n全句 True adaptation to society comes __(30)__ when the adolescent reformer\nattempts to put his ideas to work. 强调真正的社会适应不是凭空而来，而是在青少年把\n理想付诸实践时\"自动\"发生，automatically 正好表达这种条件触发、不待外力的自然发生\n关系。\n【竞争词】I) incidentally 义为\"顺便地\"，与论点焦点冲突\nincidentally 表示话题之外的附带补充或偶然发生，本句要讲的是\"适应\"这件大事的实现机制，\n绝非顺带顺便，语义直接拧反。\nN) slightly 义为\"轻微地\"，搭不上\"True adaptation\"\nTrue adaptation 已用 True 强调彻底、真正的适应，再用 slightly 表示\"轻微地达成\"逻辑自相矛\n盾，语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 comes 后缺状语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) slightly\n副词\n【词性槽】过去分词 taken 前修饰位\n【依据】\"稍稍脱离语境\"贴让步语气\n前句 Of course, youthful idealism is often courageous, and no one likes to give up\ndreams. 在为青少年辩护，作者紧接着用 Perhaps 缓和措辞，承认皮亚杰的话\"如果稍微\n脱离语境\"才显得严厉；slightly 与 Perhaps 协同营造出轻度让步的语气，正好替皮亚杰打\n圆场。\n【竞争词】I) incidentally 义为\"顺便地\"，与 out of context 不搭\nincidentally 修饰整句作\"顺便说一句\"的话题转换语，不修饰程度，也无法说\"顺便地脱离语\n境\"，搭配根本不成立。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— incidentally 看似\"-ly\"就行，实则语义反。第 31 题\nPerhaps, taken __(31)__ out of context, Piaget's statement seems harsh. 若没注意\n到 Perhaps 的让步语气，很容易随手填 incidentally；防坑一句话——见到 Perhaps、\nto some extent 等让步标志，副词优先选 slightly 这种程度词，而非 incidentally 这种插\n话词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·过去分词 taken 前修饰位"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) emphasizing\n动词\n【词性槽】was + ___ 进行时谓语\n【依据】\"皮亚杰要强调的是现实磨理想\"对应 emphasize\n本段在为皮亚杰\"开脱\"——他原话听上去严厉，但 What he was __(32)__, however, is the\nway reality can modify idealistic views. 真正想强调（emphasize）的是\"现实如何修正理\n想化观点\"。emphasize sth. 直接以观点为宾语，是最常用的学术评述动词。\n【竞争词】C) capturing 义为\"捕捉\"，多用于具体形象或注意\ncapture 常搭具体宾语（捕获敌人、捕捉瞬间、抓住注意）；说\"皮亚杰在捕捉现实修正理想的方\n式\"语义别扭，且不是\"反驳听上去严厉\"这层语境惯用动词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·was + ___ 进行时谓语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) recession\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 of 后名词位\n【依据】对接\"职业减少 + 经济艰难\"双重语境\n本句给出因果链 As careers and vocations become less available during times of\n__(33)__, adolescents may be especially hard hit.，下一句又点出 Such difficult\neconomic times may leave many adolescents __(34)__ about their roles in society.\n——\"职业减少\"加\"经济艰难\"两条线都指向 recession（经济衰退），这是唯一与因果链严\n丝合缝的名词。\n【竞争词】F) entrance \"入口/进入\"，与\"职业减少\"反向\nentrance 表示\"进入\"或\"入口\"，times of entrance 含义不明、且与下文 difficult economic\ntimes 完全脱节。\nH) existence \"存在/生存\"，本题已锁定第 27 题\nexistence 虽可表\"生存状态\"，但下一题第 27 题的语义只与 existence 严丝合缝（详见后），此\n处把它留给第 27 题；且 times of existence 也并非地道英文搭配。\nK) occupation \"职业\"，与本句 careers 内容重复\noccupation 与 careers and vocations 同义重复，且 times of occupation 在英语里多指\"被占\n领时期\"，与经济语境严重错位。\nL) promises \"承诺\"，与\"职业短缺\"无关\npromises 表\"承诺\"，times of promises 既不是固定搭配，也无法解释下文\"adolescents may\nbe especially hard hit\"的打击来源。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 of 后名词位"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) confused\n形容词\n【词性槽】leave + 宾语 + ___ 形容词补足语\n【依据】\"经济困难令青少年对自己的社会角色感到困惑\"\n本句承接\"经济衰退\"语境，Such difficult economic times may leave many adolescents\n__(34)__ about their roles in society. 说艰难时世让许多青少年对其社会角色\"困惑\"——\nconfused about one's role 是地道搭配，且与下句\"政府就业项目以激发自我价值感\"形成\n因果关联：因为先 confused，所以才需要刺激 sense of worth。\n【竞争词】B) beneficial 义为\"有益的\"，主语本应是\"事物\"非\"人\"\nbeneficial 修饰物或措施而非人；\"使青少年有益的\"语法不通，且搭配为 beneficial to，不\n接 about。\nG) excited 义为\"兴奋的\"，与\"艰难经济\"语境反向\n经济衰退令人沮丧、迷茫，不可能 excited about 自己的社会角色；语义和语气都正好反过来。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— excited 形态合规但情绪反向。第 34 题 leave many\nadolescents ___ about their roles 形态上 excited 也对，但句子语境是\"难时世+需要刺激\n价值感\"的负面语境，excited 反过来了；防坑一句话——形容词补足语先校正情感色彩，再\n校正搭配介词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·leave + 宾语 + ___ 形容词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automatically", "B) beneficial", "C) capturing", "D) confused", "E) emphasizing", "F) entrance", "G) excited", "H) existence", "I) incidentally", "J) intolerant", "K) occupation", "L) promises", "M) recession", "N) slightly", "O) undertakes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) beneficial\n形容词\n【词性槽】are not only … but also … 并列形容词位\n【依据】\"经济上有益\"对仗后半句\"激发自我价值感\"\n并列结构 not only economically beneficial but also help to stimulate the adolescent's\nsense of worth 在\"经济收益\"与\"心理价值\"两侧对仗——beneficial 表\"有益的\"恰好概括项\n目的经济正面效益；且 economically beneficial 是高频学术搭配。\n【竞争词】G) excited 义为\"激动的\"，主语应是人非项目\nexcited 通常描述人的情绪；说\"政府就业项目在经济上是激动的\"主语错位、搭配不成立，且与\nnot only … but also 的并列对仗（经济效益 + 心理效益）严重不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are not only … but a"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Examples show that both rich and poor countries exploited the environment for economic progress.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】富国穷国都为发展破坏环境\n【定位】But there are also many examples of growing wealth by trashing the environment, in rich and\npoor parts of the world alike, whether through unregulated mineral extraction, drastic water\nuse for agriculture, slash-and-burn farming, or fossil-fuel-guzzling transport.\n但是也有很多例子表明，无论是世界的富裕地区还是贫穷地区，都存在着通过破坏环境来获\n取财富增长的现象，这些做法包括无序的矿产开采、农业上的大量耗水、刀耕火种式的农\n业，以及大量消耗化石燃料的交通方式。\n【改写】both rich and poor countries ↔ in rich and poor parts of the world alike\n并列结构同义改写\nexploited the environment for economic progress ↔\ngrowing wealth by trashing the environment\n动词同义加手段目的互换\n【辨邻】 L L 段虽然区分了穷国与富国的环境问题成因——In the developing countries, most\nof the environmental problems are caused by under-development 与 In the\nindustrialised countries, environmental problems are generally related to\nindustrialisation and technological development——但它讨论的是穷富各自因何\n而污染，并没有给出为了经济进步而破坏环境的具体例子，与题干 Examples show\nthat 的举例语义不符。\nIn the developing countries, most of the environmental problems are caused by under-development", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Environmental protection and improvement benefit people all over the world.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】环境保护惠及全世界人民\n【定位】The protection and improvement of the human environment is a major issue which affects the\nwell-being of peoples and economic development throughout the world, read the final\ndeclaration from this gathering, the first of a sequence which would lead to the Rio de Janeiro\nEarth Summit in 1992 and the World Development Summit in Johannesburg three years ago.\n斯德哥尔摩这次会议的最终宣言写道：保护与改善人类环境是一项重大议题，它关系到全世\n界人民的福祉和经济发展。这次会议是此后一系列峰会的起点，其后又有 1992 年的里约地\n球峰会和三年前在约翰内斯堡举行的世界发展峰会。\n【改写】Environmental protection and improvement ↔\nThe protection and improvement of the human environment\n近乎原样复现\nbenefit people all over the world ↔ affects the well-being of peoples ... throughout the world\n动词与范围短语同义概括\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然也讲到环境与资源关系到福祉与经济发展 we must make sure we take care\nof the natural environment and resources on which our economic activity\ndepends，但这段话是出自 Gordon Brown 的发言，并不是会议宣言惠及全世界人民\n的口吻，与题干 Environmental protection and improvement benefit people all\nover the world 的宣言主张基调不一致。\nwe must make sure we take care of the natural environment and resources on which our economic\nactivity depends", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is not necessarily true that economic growth will make our world cleaner.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】经济增长未必让世界更干净\n【定位】It's simply not true that economic growth will surely make our world cleaner.\n经济增长一定会让我们的世界变得更干净，这种说法根本就不成立。\n【改写】It is not necessarily true that economic growth will make our world cleaner ↔\nIt's simply not true that economic growth will surely make our world cleaner\n否定判断加原句词面对位\n【辨邻】 M M 段陈述富国能提供更多环境改善 national parks, clean rivers, clean air and\npoison-free food，但也消耗更多自然资源 far more natural resources—fuel, water\n(all those baths and golf courses) and building materials。这是为未必更干净这一\n判断的事实佐证，但题干所断言的判断本身出自 L 段。\nThey also, however, use far more natural resources—fuel, water (all those baths and golf courses)\nand building materials", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The common theme of the UN reports is the relation between environmental protection and economic growth.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】UN 报告的共同主题\n【定位】Hunt through the reports prepared by UN agencies and development groups—many for\nconferences such as this year's Millennium Goals review—and you will find that the linkage\nbetween environmental protection and economic progress is a common thread.\n如果你翻阅由联合国机构和发展类组织准备的各种报告——其中许多是为今年的千年目标\n审议会议所准备的——你会发现：环境保护与经济进步之间的关联，是一条贯穿其中的共同\n主线。\n【改写】The common theme of the UN reports ↔\nreports prepared by UN agencies and development groups\n简称对全称\ncommon theme ↔ a common thread\n比喻同义改写\nthe relation between environmental protection and economic growth ↔\nthe linkage between environmental protection and economic progress\n同义词替换\n【辨邻】 B B 段确实涉及国际层面 the United Nations Conference on the Human\nEnvironment in Stockholm，但它讨论的是布朗发言的国际背景与历史源头，并未出\n现 UN 报告这一对象，更没有概括它们的共同主题，与题干 The common theme of\nthe UN reports 不一致。\nthe United Nations Conference on the Human Environment in Stockholm", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Development agencies disagree regarding how to tackle environment issues while ensuring economic progress.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】发展机构内部立场分歧\n【定位】It is also the reason why development agencies are not united in their view of environmental\nissues; while some, like the WRI, maintain that environmental progress needs to go hand-in-\nhand with economic development, others argue that the priority is to build a thriving\neconomy, and then use the wealth created to tackle environmental degradation.\n这也是为什么发展类机构在环境议题上立场并不统一的原因：一些机构（如世界资源研究\n所）主张环境进步必须与经济发展齐头并进；另一些机构则认为，优先要务是先建立繁荣经\n济，再用所创造的财富去治理环境退化。\n【改写】Development agencies disagree ↔\ndevelopment agencies are not united in their view of environmental issues\n否定短语等价\nhow to tackle environment issues while ensuring economic progress ↔\nenvironmental progress needs to go hand-in-hand with economic development\n句式重构表同一议题\n【辨邻】 L L 段虽然也用对比结构 In the developing countries, most of the environmental\nproblems are caused by under-development 与 In the industrialised countries,\nenvironmental problems are generally related to industrialisation and\ntechnological development，但它对比的是穷国与富国的环境问题成因，不是发展机\n构之间的策略分歧，与题干 Development agencies disagree 的对象不符。\nIn the developing countries, most of the environmental problems are caused by under-development", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is difficult to find solid evidence to prove environmental friendliness generates more profits than exploiting the natural environment.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】难以找到硬证据\n【定位】Managing ecosystems sustainably is more profitable than exploiting them, according to the\nMillennium Ecosystem Assessment. But finding hard evidence to support the thesis is not\nso easy.\n根据千年生态系统评估的研究，可持续地管理生态系统比剥削式开发更有利可图。但是，要\n找到能支持这一论断的硬证据并不容易。\n【改写】difficult to find solid evidence ↔ finding hard evidence to support the thesis is not so easy\n同义形容词加句式互换\nenvironmental friendliness generates more profits than exploiting the natural environment ↔\nManaging ecosystems sustainably is more profitable than exploiting them\n概念上位概括\n【辨邻】 G G 段虽然也涉及千年生态系统评估并讨论可持续管理，但它讨论的是结论本身 might\nbe less profitable in the short term, but certainly brings long-term rewards——\n可持续管理在长期带来回报，与题干 difficult to find solid evidence 所强调的取证困\n难语义不一致。\nmight be less profitable in the short term, but certainly brings long-term rewards\nF F 段顺着 E 段的话题接续讨论 If such an indicator exists, it is well hidden 和\nteasing out a simple economy-environment relationship would be almost\nimpossible，但它讨论的是那个理想化的全球指标几乎不可能得到，不是难以找到支\n持环境友好更赚论点的硬证据这一主张本身，题干 Q41 的源头判断仍在 E 段。\nIf such an indicator exists, it is well hidden", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Sustainable management of ecosystems will prove rewarding in the long run.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】可持续管理长期有回报\n【定位】The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, a vast four-year global study which reported its\ninitial conclusions earlier this year, found reasons to believe that managing ecosystems\nsustainably—working with nature rather than against it—might be less profitable in the short\nterm, but certainly brings long-term rewards.\n千年生态系统评估是一项历时四年的庞大全球研究，其初步结论已于今年早些时候发布。研\n究找到理由相信：可持续地管理生态系统——与自然合作而非对抗——短期内利润可能较\n低，但长期一定会带来回报。\n【改写】Sustainable management of ecosystems ↔ managing ecosystems sustainably\n名词化与动副结构互换\nwill prove rewarding in the long run ↔ certainly brings long-term rewards\n同源词族加短语同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽然也提到 Managing ecosystems sustainably is more profitable than\nexploiting them，但它的重点是很难找到硬证据，没有展开短期不赚长期有回报这一\n时间维度，与题干 in the long run 不直接对应。\nManaging ecosystems sustainably is more profitable than exploiting them, according to the\nMillennium Ecosystem Assessment\nH H 段虽然给出生态与经济关系的具体案例，但侧重点在 environmental degradation\naffects the poor more than the rich，谈的是环境退化对穷人冲击更大，不是可持续\n管理长期有回报这一论断本身。\nenvironmental degradation affects the poor more than the rich, as poorer people derive a much\nhigher proportion of their income directly from the natural resources around them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A politician noted for being cautious asserts that sustainable human development depends on the natural environment.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】审慎政治家断言可持续发展依赖自然环境\n【定位】If our economies are to flourish, if global poverty is to be eliminated and if the well-being of\nthe world's people is to be enhanced—not just in this generation but in succeeding\ngenerations—we must make sure we take care of the natural environment and resources on\nwhich our economic activity depends. That statement comes not, as you might imagine, from\na stereotypical tree-hugging, save-the-world greenie, but from Gordon Brown, a politician\nwith a reputation for rigour, thoroughness and above all, caution.\n如果我们的经济要繁荣，如果要消除全球贫困，如果世界人民的福祉要得到提升——不仅是\n这一代人，也包括后代——我们就必须确保我们照护好自然环境与资源，因为我们的经济活\n动依赖于它们。这段话并不像你想象的那样出自典型的、抱树式拯救世界的环保人士，而是\n出自戈登·布朗——一位以严谨、彻底、尤其以谨慎著称的政治家。\n【改写】A politician noted for being cautious ↔\na politician with a reputation for rigour, thoroughness and above all, caution\n同源词族加短语同义\nsustainable human development depends on the natural environment ↔\nwe must make sure we take care of the natural environment and resources on which our\neconomic activity depends\n概念上位概括\n【辨邻】 B B 段也涉及布朗发言，但内容是讲发言的国际背景与历史源头 in the run-up to the\nfive-year review of the millennium(千年的) Goals 与 The roots of his speech ...\nstretch back to 1972，并未承载可持续发展依赖自然环境这一断言本身，与题干\nasserts that sustainable human development depends on the natural\nenvironment 的内容定位不符。\nThe roots of his speech, given in March at the roundtable meeting of environment and energy\nministers from the G20 group of nations, stretch back to 1972", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Poor countries will have to bear the cost for rich nations' economic development.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】穷国为富国发展买单\n【定位】As countries become richer, they produce more greenhouse gases; and the impact of those\ngases will fall primarily in poor parts of the world.\n随着国家变得更富裕，它们排放的温室气体也更多；而这些气体造成的影响，将主要落在世\n界上较为贫穷的地区。\n【改写】rich nations' economic development ↔\nAs countries become richer, they produce more greenhouse gases\n因果同位\nPoor countries will have to bear the cost ↔\nthe impact of those gases will fall primarily in poor parts of the world\n比喻动词加范围词同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也得出环境退化对穷人冲击更大 environmental degradation affects the\npoor more than the rich，但它给出的理由是 poorer people derive a much higher\nproportion of their income directly from the natural resources around them\n——穷人对自然资源依赖更高、所以更易受冲击，并未把这种冲击归因于富国的经济\n发展，与题干 bear the cost for rich nations' economic development 的归因方向\n不同。\npoorer people derive a much higher proportion of their income directly from the natural resources\naround them\nO O 段虽然也涉及国家间财富与排放关系，并以挪威与美国对比 The average\nNorwegian is better off than the average US citizen, but contributes about half\nas much to climate change，但它只在富国之间作比较，讨论财富是否唯一变量，并\n未明示穷国承担富国代价，与题干 Q44 的承担关系不符。\nThe average Norwegian is better off than the average US citizen, but contributes about half as much\nto climate change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "One recent study warns us of the danger of the exhaustion of natural resources on Earth.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】最近一项研究警示资源耗尽\n【定位】One recent study attempted to calculate the extent of this ecological overshoot of the human\neconomy, and found that we are using 1.2 Earth's-worth of environmental goods and services\n—the implication being that at some point the debt will be called in, and all those services—\nthe things which the planet does for us for free—will grind to a halt.\n最近的一项研究试图量化这种人类经济的生态透支程度，发现我们正在消耗 1.2 个地球的环\n境产品与服务——其含义是：在某个时间点这笔生态债务将被讨回，地球免费为我们提供的\n所有服务都将停摆。\n【改写】One recent study ↔ One recent study\n原样复现\nwarns us of the danger of the exhaustion of natural resources on Earth ↔\nwe are using 1.2 Earth's-worth of environmental goods and services\n比喻还原与上位概括\n【辨邻】 I I 段虽然也涉及资源耗尽，并以 the Grand Banks fishery 与 cod(鳕鱼) 提供生动案\n例，但它讲的是单一渔业的崩溃，并没有一项最近研究警告全球自然资源耗尽的研究表\n述，与题干 One recent study 的研究对象不符。\nPerhaps the best example of boom growth and bust decline is the Grand Banks fishery", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does Colin Dixon mean by saying \"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years\" (Lines 4, Para. 1)?", "options": ["A) Interactive television advertising will become popular in 10-12 years.", "B) Interactive television advertising has been under debate for the last decade or so.", "C) Interactive television advertising is successful when incorporated into situation comedies.", "D) Interactive television advertising has not achieved the anticipated results."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干结构是 What does Colin Dixon mean by saying \"...\"，问的是某人说某句话是什么意\n思，本质是对一句原文的语义解读。\n【拆句】题干主干为 What does Colin Dixon mean by saying \"...\"，宾语从句问的是\"说这句话意\n指什么\"。by saying 是介词短语作方式状语，引号内整句是 saying 的宾语。结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Interactive television advertising, which allows viewers to use their remote controls to click\non advertisements, has been pushed for years. Nearly a decade ago it was predicted that\nviewers of \"Friends\", a popular situation comedy, would soon be able to purchase a sweater\nlike Jennifer Aniston's with a few taps on their remote control. \"It's been the year of\ninteractive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years,\" says Colin Dixon of a\ndigital-media consultancy.\n交互式电视广告——让观众用遥控器点击广告——已被力推多年。差不多十年前就有预测\n说，《老友记》这部热门情景喜剧的观众很快就能用遥控器轻点几下，买到一件像 Jennifer\nAniston 同款的毛衣。某数字媒体咨询机构的 Colin Dixon 说：\"过去十到十二年里，每一\n年都被说成是交互式电视广告之年。\"\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nwill become popular in 10-12 years 把原文的过去时 has been ... for the last ten or\ntwelve years（过去十到十二年里）扭成未来 10-12 年才会流行，时间方向反了。\nB) 无中生有\nunder debate\"被争论\"在文中找不到依据，原文只说\"被力推\"和\"被预测\"，没有提及辩\n论或争议。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文只是举 \"Friends\" 为预言中的例子，并未说交互广告\"嵌入情景喜剧\"就成功了；is\nsuccessful 与原句的反讽完全相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\n\"It's been the year of interactive television advertising for the last ten or twelve years\" ↔\nhas not achieved the anticipated results\nNearly a decade ago it was predicted that viewers ... would soon be able to purchase ↔\nthe anticipated results\n预判中\"喊了十几年没真兑现\"的两个要素——\"被预期\"和\"未达成\"——分别对应 the\nanticipated results 与 has not achieved，因此 D 即为定位句的同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the public's response to Cablevision's planned interactive TV advertising program?", "options": ["A) Pretty positive.", "B) Totally indifferent.", "C) Somewhat doubtful.", "D) Rather critical."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the public's response to Cablevision's planned interactive TV advertising\nprogram? 问的是一个明确事实——公众的反应，四个选项各对应一种态度词。\n【拆句】主干 What is the public's response，介词短语 to Cablevision's planned interactive TV\nadvertising program 作 response 的修饰，限定反应对象。planned 是过去分词作前置定\n语，修饰 program。结构无陷阱。\n【定位】So the news that Cablevision, an American cable company, was rolling out interactive\nadvertisements to all its customers on October 6th was greeted with some skepticism.\n于是，美国有线电视公司 Cablevision 将于 10 月 6 日向所有客户推出交互广告这件事，遭\n到了一些怀疑。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nPretty positive 与原文 skepticism 极性相反；原文是负面态度。\nB) 与原文相反\nTotally indifferent 意为\"完全漠不关心\"，但原文明确有 skepticism 这一反应，绝非\n漠然。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwas greeted with some skepticism ↔ Somewhat doubtful\n预判中\"怀疑但不强烈\"两个特征——语义\"怀疑\"和程度\"一些\"——分别落到 doubtful 与\nSomewhat，故选 C。\nD) 偷换概念\nRather critical\"相当批评\"比原文 some skepticism\"一些怀疑\"程度重；some≠rather，\nskepticism 是\"怀疑\"不是\"批评\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the impact of the wide use of digital video recorders on TV advertising?", "options": ["A) It has made TV advertising easily accessible to viewers.", "B) It helps advertisers to measure the click-through rates.", "C) It has placed TV advertising at a great disadvantage.", "D) It enables viewers to check the sales items with ease."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the impact of the wide use of digital video recorders on TV advertising? 直\n接问\"X 对 Y 的影响是什么\"，是明确的事实查询。\n【拆句】主干 What is the impact；of 短语 of the wide use of digital video recorders 限定\nimpact 的来源，on 短语 on TV advertising 限定影响对象。两层介词修饰，方向清晰：A\n影响 B，问 A 对 B 的作用。\n【定位】The popularization of digital video recorders has caused advertisers to worry that their\ncommercials will be skipped.\n数字录像机的普及让广告商担心他们的广告会被跳过。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\neasily accessible to viewers 是正向描述\"更容易被观众接触到\"，与原文广告被跳的负\n面后果完全相反。\nB) 张冠李戴\nhelps advertisers to measure the click-through rates 把段中介绍 the Internet,\nwhich is cheaper and offers concrete measurements like click-through rates 这件\n互联网的事，扣到 digital video recorders 头上。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhas caused advertisers to worry that their commercials will be skipped ↔\nplaced TV advertising at a great disadvantage\nhas caused ↔ has ... placed\n预判中\"广告处境变差\"与选项 placed TV advertising at a great disadvantage 表达完\n全一致，选 C。\nD) 无中生有\nenables viewers to check the sales items with ease 在原文找不到对应：原文没有说\nDVR 让观众查看促销商品。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn about Unilever's interactive campaign?", "options": ["A) It proves the advantage of TV advertising.", "B) It has done well in engaging the viewers.", "C) It helps attract investments in the company.", "D) It has boosted the TV advertising industry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about Unilever's interactive campaign? 是\"关于这件事我们了解到什\n么\"——回到原文该例证句直接读取。\n【拆句】What do we learn about Unilever's interactive campaign? 主干 What do we learn，\nabout 介词短语指明话题对象。Unilever's 是名词所有格，限定 campaign 的归属。结构无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Unilever recently ran an interactive campaign for its Axe deodorant(除臭剂), which kept\nviewers engaged for more than three minutes on average.\n联合利华最近为旗下 Axe 除臭剂做了一个交互广告活动，它平均让观众持续投入超过三\n分钟。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nproves the advantage of TV advertising 把话题从\"Unilever 这次活动如何\"扩大成\"电\n视广告整体优势\"；原句讨论的只是这一次具体的交互广告效果，不证明 TV 广告整体\n优势。\nB) ✓ 正确\nkept viewers engaged for more than three minutes on average ↔\ndone well in engaging the viewers\nfor more than three minutes on average ↔ done well\n预判中\"在吸引观众投入上效果好\"与 done well in engaging the viewers 完全一致，\n选 B。\nC) 无中生有\nhelps attract investments in the company 谈\"吸引对公司的投资\"，原文只字未提\n投资。\nD) 过度推断\nhas boosted the TV advertising industry 把单次广告的效果扩大成\"带动了整个电视\n广告行业\"；原文只是单一例证，没有行业层面推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How does the author view the hitherto high click-through rates?", "options": ["A) They may be due to the novel way of advertising.", "B) They signify the popularity of interactive advertising.", "C) They point to the growing curiosity of TV viewers.", "D) They indicate the future direction of media reform."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】How does the author view the hitherto high click-through rates? 是\"作者如何看待\nX\"，标准的态度评判题。\n【拆句】How does the author view the hitherto high click-through rates? 主干 How does the\nauthor view X。hitherto（到目前为止）是副词修饰形容词 high，强调\"迄今为止还高\"的\n隐含时态张力——暗示作者可能不看好这个高数据的持续性。\n【定位】Click-through rates have been high so far (around 3-4%, compared with less than 0.3%\nonline), but that may be a result of the novelty.\n迄今点击通过率一直较高（约 3-4%，相比之下网上不到 0.3%），但这可能只是新鲜感造\n成的结果。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbut that may be a result of the novelty ↔ may be due to the novel way of advertising\n预判中\"作者怀疑这只是新鲜感造成\"两要素——情态 may 和原因 novelty——分别对应\nmay be due to 与 the novel way of advertising，故选 A。\nB) 过度推断\nsignify the popularity of interactive advertising 把高点击率直接读成\"交互广\n告流行\"，但作者通篇态度是质疑：Interactive ads and viewers might not go\nwell together。\nC) 偷换概念\npoint to the growing curiosity of TV viewers 把\"新鲜感\"偷换成\"日益增长的好奇\n心\"；原文只说新鲜，而且暗示\"会过去\"，并非\"growing\"。\nD) 无中生有\nindicate the future direction of media reform 把高点击率说成\"媒体改革的未来方\n向\"，原文从未谈媒体改革。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think is the root cause of mass unemployment in America?", "options": ["A) Corporate mismanagement.", "B) Insufficient demand.", "C) Technological advances.", "D) Workers' slow adaptation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think is the root cause 问的是作者明示的判断，原文必有一句直\n白表态，属于细节题而非推断题。\n【拆句】What does the author think is the root cause of mass unemployment in America?\n主干是 What does the author think，宾语从句省略 that，从句主干 the root cause of\nmass unemployment in America is what；难点仅在 think 后接的是嵌套疑问句，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】On the contrary, all the facts suggest that high unemployment in America is the result of\ninadequate demand.\n恰恰相反，所有事实都表明，美国的高失业率是需求不足造成的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nCorporate mismanagement.\"企业管理失当\"在原文从未出现，纯属考生常识联想。\nB) ✓ 正确\ninadequate demand ↔ Insufficient demand.\n预判\"需求不够\"与 Insufficient demand. 字面一致，无需扩展即可锁定。\nC) 无中生有\nTechnological advances. 是经济类话题常见的\"标准嫌疑人\"，但本文未提任何技术变\n革，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nWorkers' slow adaptation. 复述的是 they're in the wrong places, or they have the\nwrong skills 这一专家立场，而作者在 structural unemployment is a fake problem\n已明确否定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author think of the experts' claim concerning unemployment?", "options": ["A) Self-evident.", "B) Thought-provoking.", "C) Irrational.", "D) Groundless."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the experts' claim 中 think of 直接问态度/评价，选项\nSelf-evident.、Groundless. 都是评价性形容词，进一步坐实态度题。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the experts' claim concerning unemployment? 主干\nWhat does the author think of …；concerning unemployment 作 claim 的后置修饰，\n等于 about unemployment；句式平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】In other words, structural unemployment is a fake problem, which mainly serves as an excuse\nfor not pursuing real solutions.\n换句话说，结构性失业是个伪问题，它的主要作用不过是为\"不去追求真正解决方案\"提供\n借口。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nSelf-evident.（不言自明、显而易见）暗示作者认同专家说法，与 structural\nunemployment is a fake problem 完全对立。\nB) 与原文相反\nThought-provoking. 意为\"发人深思\"，隐含一定肯定，与作者的全面否定态度不符。\nC) 偷换概念\nIrrational.\"非理性\"指向\"逻辑错乱、无道理\"，作者却始终在指责对方 there's no\nevidence whatsoever\"无证据\"，攻击点错位，属偷换概念。\nD) ✓ 正确\na fake problem ↔ Groundless.\nthere's no evidence whatsoever to back these claims ↔ Groundless.\n全文极性词都指向\"无事实支撑\"，Groundless. 恰是这一极性的形容词概括。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say helped bring down unemployment during the Great Depression?", "options": ["A) The booming defense industry.", "B) The wise heads' benefit package.", "C) Nationwide training of workers.", "D) Thorough restructuring of industries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say helped bring down unemployment during the Great\nDepression 限定在大萧条这一时段问\"是什么帮忙降的失业\"，需要的就是原文那一句的客\n观转述。\n【拆句】What does the author say helped bring down unemployment during the Great\nDepression? What 既是宾语从句主语又是 helped 的逻辑主语，相当于 What helped\nbring down unemployment（the author says）；during 短语作时间状语，无陷阱。\n【定位】A few years later, a large defense buildup finally provided a fiscal stimulus adequate to the\neconomy's needs—and suddenly industry was eager to employ those \"unadaptable and\nuntrained\" workers.\n几年后，一次大规模的国防扩张终于带来了与经济需要相称的财政刺激——产业界突然就乐\n于雇用那些\"难以适应、未受训练\"的工人了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na large defense buildup ↔ The booming defense industry.\n预判\"国防扩张刺激经济\"与 The booming defense industry. 字面对齐，逻辑一步\n即到。\nB) 无中生有\nThe wise heads' benefit package. 把 All the wise heads agree 的讽刺称呼当成具体\n人物，且\"福利方案\"原文从未出现，属无中生有。\nC) 与原文相反\nNationwide training of workers. 与作者立场相反——作者强调 We aren't suffering\nfrom a shortage of needed skills，培训根本不是降失业的钥匙。\nD) 无中生有\nThorough restructuring of industries.\"产业的彻底重组\"在原文中从未出现，且与作\n者\"需求拉动\"的诊断方向不符。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What has caused claims of huge structural problems to multiply?", "options": ["A) Powerful opposition to government's stimulus efforts.", "B) Very Serious People's attempt to cripple the economy.", "C) Evidence gathered from many sectors of the industries.", "D) Economists' failure to detect the problems in time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What has caused … 是直接问\"是什么导致 X\"的因果问句，原文几乎必然有一\n句\"because/that is why\"形式的明示因果，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】What has caused claims of huge structural problems to multiply? 主干 What has\ncaused X to do；现在完成时强调结果延续到现在，to multiply 是 cause 后的不定式宾\n补，无陷阱。\n【定位】But now, as then, powerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government\naction on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy. And that, fundamentally, is why claims\nthat we face huge structural problems have been multiplying: they offer a reason to do\nnothing about the mass unemployment that is crippling our economy and our society.\n但今天和当年一样，强势力量在意识形态上反对政府以足够规模采取行动来重启经济。从根\n本上说，这就是为何\"我们面临巨大结构性问题\"这类说法越来越多——它们为\"不对正在摧\n垮经济和社会的大规模失业采取任何行动\"提供了借口。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\npowerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action ↔\nPowerful opposition to government's stimulus efforts.\ngovernment action on a sufficient scale to jump-start the economy ↔\ngovernment's stimulus efforts\n预判\"强势力量反对政府出手\"与 Powerful opposition to government's stimulus\nefforts. 字面同义，直接锁定。\nB) 张冠李戴\nVery Serious People's attempt to cripple the economy. 把原文 mass\nunemployment that is crippling our economy 中\"摧垮经济\"的施动者从\"失业\"偷换为\nVery Serious People，且原文也没说他们\"企图\"摧垮经济。\nC) 与原文相反\nEvidence gathered from many sectors of the industries. 与作者立场矛盾——P3 的\n全行业证据恰恰被作者用来推翻\"结构性\"说法，证据不会让\"结构性说法\"增多，而是反向\n打击。\nD) 无中生有\nEconomists' failure to detect the problems in time. 原文没有任何一句指控\"经济学\n家未能及时发现\"，作者批评的是\"明知证据不足却仍鼓吹结构性\"，性质完全不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is the author's purpose in writing the passage?", "options": ["A) To testify to the experts' analysis of America's problems.", "B) To offer a feasible solution to the structural unemployment.", "C) To show the urgent need for the government to take action.", "D) To alert American workers to the urgency for adaptation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What is the author's purpose in writing the passage? 中 purpose in writing 是典型主\n旨题信号，正确项要能覆盖全文论证链而非某一段。\n【拆句】What is the author's purpose in writing the passage? 主干 What is the purpose；in\nwriting the passage 是介词短语作 purpose 的后置定语，等同于 of writing；无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTo testify to the experts' analysis of America's problems.\"为专家分析作证\"与作者立\n场完全相反——他全文都在反驳 structural unemployment is a fake problem。\nB) 偷换概念\nTo offer a feasible solution to the structural unemployment. 把作者眼中根本不存在\n的问题（fake problem）当作要解决的对象，且作者也没给出具体方案，属\"以伪问题为\n前提\"的偷换概念。\nC) ✓ 正确\naction is desperately needed ↔ To show the urgent need for the government to take action.\npowerful forces are ideologically opposed to the whole idea of government action ↔\nfor the government to take action\n预判\"呼吁政府赶紧行动\"与 To show the urgent need for the government to take\naction. 完全对齐，且 C 同时覆盖前 6 段的\"破谬\"和末段的\"呼吁\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\nTo alert American workers to the urgency for adaptation. 把呼吁对象从\"政府\"偷换\n成\"工人\"，且 adaptation 正是作者反对的说法（unadaptable and untrained 是被嘲\n讽的话术）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
